BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Year of the Cannibals (I cannibali) in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260326
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260327
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be22d-ed44-b369-c41f-cb6b6909f693@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Locarno Film Festival Artistic Director Giona A. Na
 zzaro\n\nThursday\, March 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 28m\n\n1969 | 88 min. | Italy | Color | Italian wi
 th English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Liliana Cavani  
 \nWRITTEN BY: Liliana Cavani\, Italo Moscati  \nWITH: Britt Ekland\, Pier
 re Clémenti\, Tomas Milian\, Delia Boccardo \n4K restoration realized by 
 the Locarno Film Festival as part of Locarno Heritage in collaboration wit
 h Cinegrell Lab Zurich.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKnown internationally for h
 er divisive\, erotic wartime drama The Night Porter (1974)\, Italian filmm
 aker Liliana Cavani got her start in biographical television dramas with f
 ilms about Saint Francis of Assisi and the astronomer Galileo. Her memorab
 le theatrical feature debut is loosely based on Sophocles’s Antigone\, a
  play in which the titular heroine attempts to bury her brother despite la
 ws forbidding it. Set in a totalitarian Milan\, The Year of the Cannibals 
 opens on streets strewn with the corpses of rebels\, their “dead bodies 
 serv[ing] as an example to prevent more death.” Grieving her brother\, w
 hose body lies in front of a bar\, Cavani’s modern-day Antigone (Britt E
 kland) seeks help to defy the burial ban\, finding an ally in a foreigner 
 (Pierre Clémenti). Featuring a score by the incomparable Ennio Morricone 
 and with editing by Sergio Leone regular Nino Baragli\, this shocking\, no
 -holds-barred political satire is primed for rediscovery in its newly rest
 ored 4K edition. \nProgrammed and note by K.J. Relth-Miller.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-year-of-the-cannibals-i-
 cannibali-in-4-k-019be22d-ed44-b369-c41f-cb6b6909f693
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gunvor Nelson Tribute II: Moons Pool
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260327
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260328
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be219-d804-b817-96ac-a119453fa67b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Steve Anker\n\nFriday\, March 27\, 2026\n\nLocation
 : Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 15m\n\nSchmeerguntz  \nDIR
 ECTED BY: Gunvor Nelson\, Dorothy Wiley  \n1966. 15 min. USA. Black-and-Wh
 ite. Sound. 16mm \nMy Name is Oona \n1969. 10 min. USA. Black-and-White. E
 nglish\, Swedish. 16mm \nFog Pumas \nDIRECTED BY: Gunvor Nelson\, Dorothy 
 Wiley \n1967. 25 min. USA. Black-and-White/Color. English. 16mm \nMoons Po
 ol \n1973. 15 min. USA. Color. English. Digital \nSnowdrift (aka Snowstorm
 ) \n2001. 9 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital  \nTotal program runtime: appr
 ox. 74 min.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA pioneer of personal cinema and femini
 st film\, Gunvor Nelson produced innovative work combining painting\, coll
 age\, and sound experimentation with humor\, resistance\, intimacy\, and t
 actile sensation. Her early films frequently reflect the qualities\, thoug
 hts\, and voices of women in the 1960s and 1970s\; homes\, daughters\, and
  mothers are particularly important themes. Moving from painting to still 
 photography and from analog film to digital media\, her work delves deeper
  into the texture of life and the past\, uncovering an intuitive yet delic
 ate sensibility that retreats from the real world into another\, imaginati
 vely reconstructed one. During her time teaching in San Francisco\, Nelson
  inspired countless experimental filmmakers and artists. After returning t
 o Sweden\, she continued to explore new audiovisual languages\, leaving us
  with a rich legacy. \nThe program trilogy is co-presented by LA Filmforum
 \, Rotations\, the Academy Museum\, and UCLA Film & Television Archive on 
 March 22\, 27\, and 28. The screenings begin with introductions by film sc
 holar Steve Anker and a prerecorded video with filmmaker Lynne Sachs. \nTh
 is program covers a range of Nelson’s works\, from her debut film\, the 
 feminist classic Schmeerguntz (1966) co-directed with Dorothy Wiley\, to t
 he late abstract video art Snowdrift (aka Snowstorm\, 2001)\, with several
  other essential and influential masterpieces created during the years in 
 between. \nAll films directed by Gunvor Nelson unless otherwise noted. \nP
 rogrammed and notes by Cherlyn Hsing-Hsin Liu and Steve Anker. With video 
 introduction from filmmaker Lynne Sachs.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/gunvor-nelson-tribute-ii-moo
 ns-pool-019be219-d804-b817-96ac-a119453fa67b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gallery Spotlight: Archiving the Academy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260327T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260327T133000
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019b8fa0-4748-7413-3d33-37b9d023a7fe@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, March 27\, 2026 at Noon–1pm\n\nLocation: Sidney Poi
 tier Grand Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\n\nWithout careful preservation\, 
 many precious and enlightening objects from the film industry would be los
 t to time. Across all 19 of our branches\, ranging from acting to writing 
 to artist representation\, the Academy strives to preserve and document ra
 re photographs\, one-of-a-kind objects\, iconic costumes\, props and more.
  \nIn this spotlight event\, Research Assistant Josue Lopez and Academy Co
 llection and Preservation Executive Vice President Matt Severson share the
  Academy's efforts to preserve and nurture the legacy of film history. \nV
 ice President of Curatorial Affairs Doris Berger moderates.\nAbout Gallery
  SpotlightsGallery Spotlights feature special guests who bring fresh insig
 hts to our exhibitions. Join us for revealing monthly conversations connec
 ting the objects in our galleries to the art and history of moviemaking.\n
 \nIf you have any questions or need assistance planning your visit\, pleas
 e email museumeducation@oscars.org.
LOCATION:Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/gallery-spotlight-archiving-
 the-academy-019b8fa0-4748-7413-3d33-37b9d023a7fe
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The 400 Blows in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260327
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260328
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0c70-691e-6ad6-dff2-61eadc676427@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, March 27\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 39m\n\n1959 | 99 min. | France | B&W | Scope | Fr
 ench with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: François 
 Truffaut  \nWRITTEN BY: François Truffaut\, Marcel Moussy  \nWITH: Jean
 -Pierre Léaud\, Claire Maurier\, Albert Rémy\, Guy Decomble\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nAfter gaining fame\, and sometimes notoriety\, as a critic at t
 he French film magazine Cahiers du Cinéma\, François Truffaut made his d
 irectorial debut with The 400 Blows. Jean-Pierre Léaud stars as Antoine D
 oinel in this semiautobiographical film inspired by the delinquency of Tru
 ffaut’s youth\, which was filled with an early love of cinema. One of th
 e most influential films of the French New Wave movement\, it earned Truff
 aut a Best Director award at the Cannes Film Festival\, and his script\, c
 owritten with Marcel Moussy\, was nominated for Best Original Screenplay a
 t the 32nd Academy Awards.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-400-blows-019c0c70-691e-
 6ad6-dff2-61eadc676427
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Beyond Encounters with Ben Burtt and WALL-E
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260328T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260328T153000
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c7328-6ad6-45ed-a86d-0cb1a00337d9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, March 28\, 2026 at 2pm\n\nLocation: Level 3\n\nCost
 : Free with general admission\n\nType: Conversations\n\nGet an inside look
  at the Inventing Worlds and Characters: Encounters gallery before it take
 s off from the museum. Soar through the galaxy with Academy Award–winner
  Ben Burtt as he discusses cinema sound design as an art form and his cont
 ributions to the iconic audio landscapes of franchises such as Star Wars a
 nd Indiana Jones.\nBefore or after the tour\, meet fellow space traveler W
 ALL-E for an exclusive meet and greet in the Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby.\n
 Schedule\n11:30am–1:30pm | WALL-E Meet and Greet | Sidney Poitier Grand 
 Lobby\nTake photos with a fan-built version of your favorite trash-collect
 ing robot. General admission ticket not required.\n2–3pm | Beyond Encoun
 ters with Ben Burtt | Level 3 | Inventing Worlds and Characters: Encounter
 s \n3:30–4:30pm | WALL-E Meet and Greet | Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby\nTa
 ke photos with a fan-built version of your favorite trash-collecting robot
 . General admission ticket not required.
LOCATION:Level 3
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/beyond-encounters-with-ben-b
 urtt-019c7328-6ad6-45ed-a86d-0cb1a00337d9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:From Russia With Love in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260328
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260329
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be222-e3ce-d3b4-7a35-cf60d85d1a1d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, March 28\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 55m\n\n1963 | 115 min. | UK | Color | English |
  Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Terence Young \nWRITTEN BY: Richard Maib
 aum \nADAPTATION: Johanna Harwood \nWITH: Sean Connery\, Pedro Armendáriz
 \, Robert Shaw\, Daniela Bianchi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSean Connery made 
 his second appearance as agent 007 in this gripping film version of Ian Fl
 eming’s novel\, one of the highpoints of the long-running James Bond mov
 ie series. Bond finds himself involved in a twisty mission in Istanbul whe
 n a beautiful Russian (Daniela Bianchi) offers him the chance to obtain a 
 much-desired code-breaking device\, leading to betrayal and cross-continen
 tal danger. \nConnery’s friend and TV costar Robert Shaw made a big impr
 ession with audiences via his chilling portrayal of the assassin Donald Gr
 ant\, one of the most threatening of all Bond villains and a key step in t
 he actor’s path to international stardom.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/from-russia-with-love-019be2
 22-e3ce-d3b4-7a35-cf60d85d1a1d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Neighbor Totoro
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260328
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260329
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21e-0312-4603-5a01-899f52198f80@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, March 28\, 2026\n\nLocation
 : Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 26m\n\n1988 | 86 min. |
  Japan | Color | English (Dubbed) | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Ha
 yao Miyazaki\nWITH: Tim Daly\, Dakota Fanning\, Elle Fanning\, Lea Salonga
 \n\nType: Screenings\n\nSatsuki and her little sister\, Mei\, move to the 
 country in the dog days of summer with their father to be closer to the ho
 spital where their mother is convalescing. As the sisters explore the cica
 da-buzzing countryside\, they discover that their new home is filled with 
 gentle forest spirits who can only be seen by children. The film’s stunn
 ing hand-drawn animation style and childlike whimsy weaves a heartwarming 
 tale that is widely regarded as one of the best animated films of all time
 . My Neighbor Totoro includes some of Hayao Miyazaki’s most beloved crea
 tions such as the soot gremlins\, the Cat Bus\, and of course the Cheshire
 -grinned Totoro himself\, whose iconic profile appears on every Studio Ghi
 bli release to this day.\nOn the last Saturday of every month\, the museum
 's Family Matinee is an accommodative screening where we offer open captio
 ning\, keep the theater dimly lit\, and maintain a lower volume specifical
 ly for neurodivergent viewers.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/my-neighbor-totoro-019be21e-
 0312-4603-5a01-899f52198f80
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seven Samurai (Shichinin no samura) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260328
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260329
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0628-be60-dae6-060a-dd3d68c1942c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, March 28\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 3h 27m\n\n1954 | 207 min. | Japan | Black-and-Whit
 e | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira
  Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Hideo Oguni \
 nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Takashi Shimura\, Keiko Tsushima\, Yukiko Shimazak
 i \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\
 nIn 16th-century Japan\, a small farming village faces the threat of a ban
 dit gang plundering their next harvest. Following the advice of Gisaku\, t
 he community elder\, the villagers embark on a quest to recruit samurai to
  help defend their village. Led by the disciplined and skillful Kambei (Ta
 kashi Shimura)\, the samurai train the townsfolk to prepare for battle. Th
 is action-packed epic was highly influential upon its release\, spawning s
 everal adaptations and remakes\, most notably The Magnificent Seven (1960)
 . Seven Samurai garnered two Academy Award nominations for Best Art Direct
 ion (black-and-white)\, and Best Costume Design (black-and-white).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/seven-samurai-shichinin-no-s
 amurai-in-35-mm-019c0628-be60-dae6-060a-dd3d68c1942c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Marty in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260329
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be224-f2f5-e0b9-f287-f7330bdf12ee@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, March 29\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1955 | 93 min. | USA | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Delbert Mann \nWRITTEN BY: Paddy 
 Chayefsky \nWITH: Ernest Borgnine\, Betsy Blair\, Esther Minciotti\, Joe 
 Mantell\n\nType: Screenings\n\nErnest Borgnine had largely been stereotype
 d in villainous supporting roles (memorably in From Here to Eternity\, 195
 3) when director Delbert Mann and casting supervisor Betty Pagel chose him
  to star in the film version of Paddy Chayefsky’s acclaimed TV play—wh
 ich had starred Rod Steiger and Nancy Marchand/earned him the Best Actor O
 scar and helped launch a varied career that lasted into the 21st century. 
 \nWinner of Best Picture (Harold Hecht\, producer)\, Directing (Mann)\, an
 d Screenplay (Chayefsky)\, the film was also nominated for its black-and-w
 hite art direction and black-and-white cinematography\, and for the suppor
 ting performances of Joe Mantell as Marty’s friend and Betsy Blair as Ma
 rty’s love interest.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/marty-019be224-f2f5-e0b9-f28
 7-f7330bdf12ee
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rashomon in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260329
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0629-6d2a-e47b-6596-83442a04f70d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, March 29\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 28m\n\n1950 | 88 min. | Japan | Black-and-White |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Akira K
 urosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Shinobu Hashimoto \nWITH: Toshiro M
 ifune\, Machiko Kyo\, Masayuki Mori\, Takashi Shimura\nRestored 35mm relea
 se print made in 2008. Restored by The Academy Film Archive\, The National
  Film Center of the National Museum of Modern Art\, Tokyo and Kadokawa Pic
 tures\, Inc.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAkira Kurosawa’s classic\, time-shif
 ting drama about a fatal encounter between a samurai\, his wife\, and a ba
 ndit in a desolate forest\, and the accounts of four witnesses to the crim
 e\, revolutionized film language. Kurosawa’s narrative structure of exhi
 biting a single incident through subjective yet conflicting viewpoints int
 roduced the “Rashomon effect” into popular culture. Rashomon received 
 an Honorary Foreign Language Film Oscar from the Academy in 1951. With str
 iking black-and-white cinematography by Kazuo Miyagawa and Oscar-nominated
  Art Direction (black-and-white)\, Rashomon remains one of global cinema
 ’s most influential films.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/rashomon-in-35-mm-019c0629-6
 d2a-e47b-6596-83442a04f70d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Welcome II the Terrordome with Ashley Clark and Ngozi Onwurah
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260331
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be232-b8d5-82fb-2cc1-d5b4c041dab8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Restoration world premiere\n\nMonday\, March 30\, 2026\n\nLoca
 tion: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1995 | 95 mi
 n. | UK | Color | English | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Ngozi On
 wurah  \nWITH: Suzette Llewellyn\, Saffron Burrows\, Felix Joseph\, Valen
 tine Nonyela\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWith her 1995 feature Welcome II the T
 errordome\, Ngozi Onwurah became the first Black British female director t
 o have a film released theatrically in the UK. In a future ghetto riddled 
 with drugs and ruled by gangs\, a grieving mother’s anger ignites a viol
 ent uprising. Featuring a hip-hop score that functions as a Greek chorus\,
  this uncompromising film addresses the legacy of slavery and ongoing terr
 or of police and state violence. Named after a blistering 1990 track by Pu
 blic Enemy\, the film\, also written by Onwurah\, stands out not only for 
 its vividly disturbing content but also for its uniqueness in the predomin
 antly white and male landscapes of cyberpunk and British cinema. \nNote by
  Ashley Clark\, adapted from his essay in the Museum’s catalog Cyberpunk
 : Envisioning Possible Futures Through Cinema (2024).  \nClark and direct
 or Onwurah join us in conversation about this singular work.\nIn a separat
 ely ticketed event\, Clark also signs copies of his new book\, The World o
 f Black Film: A Journey Through Cinematic Blackness in 100 Films (Laurence
  King\, 2026). Copies will be available to purchase in advance of the prog
 ram.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/welcome-ii-the-terrordome-wi
 th-ashley-clark-019be232-b8d5-82fb-2cc1-d5b4c041dab8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Psycho
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260330
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260331
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be236-4fe2-eaa6-db24-ca2586dcc299@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, March 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1960 | 109 min. | USA | Black-and-White | 
 English | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock  \nWRITTEN BY: Joseph S
 tefano\nWITH: Anthony Perkins\, Vera Miles\, John Gavin\, Janet Leigh\n\nT
 ype: Screenings\n\n“When I was a kid\,” director Bong Joon Ho writes i
 n the catalog for his current exhibition at the Academy Museum\, “I watc
 hed so many Alfred Hitchcock movies on Korean television. Hitchcock is sti
 ll one of my biggest inspirations. I’ve seen Psycho more than 50 times.
 ” This psychological thriller opens with Marion Crane (Janet Leigh) absc
 onding with $40\,000 cash from her employer in Phoenix to deliver to her d
 ebt-ridden fiancé in California. Along the way she stops at the Bates Mot
 el\, where an encounter with curious innkeeper Norman (Anthony Perkins) be
 gets one of the most harrowing and famous scenes in cinema history. An Ita
 lian poster from Bong’s personal collection hangs in the exhibition\, on
  view on Level 2\, and he included the title on his 2022 Sight & Sound lis
 t as one of 10 films that he considers the greatest of all time.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/psycho-019be236-4fe2-eaa6-db
 24-ca2586dcc299
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Fair Lady in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260401
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260402
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be20e-8af2-702b-afe5-7e7845b8cb41@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Governor Gerald Quist \nSelected by the Mak
 eup Artists and Hairstylists Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 1\, 2026\n\nLocat
 ion: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 50m\n\n1964 | 170 mi
 n. | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated G | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Georg
 e Cukor \nWRITTEN BY: Alan Jay Lerner \nWITH: Audrey Hepburn\, Rex Harriso
 n\, Stanley Holloway\, Theodore Bikel\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis lavish f
 ilm version of the hit Broadway musical from composer Frederick Loewe and 
 lyricist/book writer Alan Jay Lerner—based on George Bernard Shaw’s cl
 assic play Pygmalion—earned eight Oscars\, including Best Picture. Rex H
 arrison reprised his Tony Award-winning performance as the irascible Profe
 ssor Henry Higgins for the big screen and earned the Best Actor award. Aud
 rey Hepburn is her typically enchanting self as his pupil Eliza Doolittle 
 (singing voice provided by Marni Nixon)\, her transformation aided by make
 up supervisor Gordon Bau and supervising hair stylist Jean Burt Reilly.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/my-fair-lady-in-4-k-019be20e
 -8af2-702b-afe5-7e7845b8cb41
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
