UID:019be236-0eb9-f843-f472-84e8cb2c43ac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, April 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1960 | 109 min. | USA | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock  \nWRITTEN BY: Joseph St
 efano  \nWITH: Anthony Perkins\, Vera Miles\, John Gavin\, Janet Leigh\n\
 nType: Screenings\n\n“When I was a kid\,” director Bong Joon Ho writes
  in the catalog for his current exhibition at the Academy Museum\, “I wa
 tched so many Alfred Hitchcock movies on Korean television. Hitchcock is s
 till one of my biggest inspirations. I’ve seen Psycho more than 50 times
 .” This psychological thriller opens with Marion Crane (Janet Leigh) abs
 conding with $40\,000 cash from her employer in Phoenix to deliver to her 
 debt-ridden fiancé in California. Along the way she stops at the Bates Mo
 tel\, where an encounter with curious innkeeper Norman (Anthony Perkins) b
 egets one of the most harrowing and famous scenes in cinema history. An It
 alian poster from Bong’s personal collection hangs in the exhibition\, o
 n view on Level 2\, and he included the title on his 2022 Sight & Sound li
 st as one of 10 films that he considers the greatest of all time.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/psycho-019be236-0eb9-f843-f4
 72-84e8cb2c43ac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ikiru (To Live) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260403
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260404
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c062a-f565-75f5-4be0-16278de57735@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, April 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 23m\n\n1952 | 143 min. | Japan | Black-and-White |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Akira K
 urosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Hideo Oguni \nW
 ITH: Takashi Shimura\, Nobuo Kaneko\, Shin’ichi Himori\, Haruo Tanaka\n\
 nType: Screenings\n\nAkira Kurosawa explores embracing life through death 
 in aging bureaucrat Kanji Watanabe (Takashi Shimura)\, who spends his days
  on autopilot at his monotonous desk job. When Kanji discovers that he has
  terminal stomach cancer\, he questions the meaning of life\, searching fo
 r a reason to continue living with the little time he has left. Shimura’
 s quietly expressive and heartbreaking portrayal of Watanabe is widely con
 sidered a career-defining role for the actor. The film received the Specia
 l Prize of the Senate of Berlin at the 1954 Berlin International Film Fest
 ival.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/ikiru-to-live-in-35-mm-019c0
 62a-f565-75f5-4be0-16278de57735
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Lupin the 3rd: The Castle of Cagliostro
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260404
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21e-9c42-b967-5e8b-23fcb32e8be6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 4\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1979 | 110 min. | Japan | Color | English
  (Dubbed) | Rated PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWRITTEN BY: Hay
 ao Miyazaki\, Haruya Yamazaki \nWITH: Bob Bergen\, Joan Carol O’Connell\
 , Michael McConnohie\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s debut featu
 re revisits a character he directed for television―gentleman thief Lupin
  the 3rd. Grandson to Maurice Leblanc’s French literary master thief Ars
 ène Lupin\, Lupin the 3rd is a Swinging Sixties-style\, James Bond-like c
 harmer who steals for thrills. Stuck with counterfeit bills after a Monaco
  casino heist\, Lupin and his sidekicks hightail it to the mysterious prin
 cipality of Cagliostro to find the source of the fake money. In the proces
 s\, the trio becomes embroiled in palace intrigue between Count Cagliostro
  and Princess Clarisse. An energetic caper\, Lupin the 3rd: The Castle of 
 Cagliostro is a stylish start to a brilliant oeuvre.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/lupin-the-3rd-the-castle-of-
 cagliostro-019be21e-9c42-b967-5e8b-23fcb32e8be6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Follow That Bird with director Ken Kwapis
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260404
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be230-8d08-76b5-4f40-740527381e8e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: director Ken Kwapis and Debra Spinney\n\nSaturday\,
  April 4\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime:
  1h 28m\n\n1985 | 88 min. | USA | Color | English\, American Sign Language
  | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Ken Kwapis  \nWRITTEN BY: Tony Geiss\, Ju
 dy Freudberg  \nWITH: Carroll Spinney\, Jim Henson\, Frank Oz\, Richard H
 unt\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSince its debut on National Education Televisio
 n in 1969\, Sesame Street has raised generations of children with its play
 ful combination of pure entertainment and early education segments\, achie
 ved through a mix of animation\, live actors\, and skillful puppetry. The 
 first feature-length theatrical film to bring this small-screen community 
 to the big screen\, Follow That Bird is a freewheeling road musical that f
 ollows 8-foot-tall avian Big Bird (Carroll Spinney in a heart-melting perf
 ormance) from his placement with a foster family to his trek back to his r
 eal home on Sesame Street. \nWith delightful guest appearances by Paul Bar
 tel\, Sandra Bernhard\, John Candy\, Chevy Chase\, and Waylon Jennings\, a
 long with show regulars Oscar the Grouch\, Mr. Snuffleupagus\, Grover\, Be
 rt and Ernie\, and Elmo\, this seminal children’s film about family\, be
 longing\, and growth has something for kids and adults alike.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/follow-that-bird-019be230-8d
 08-76b5-4f40-740527381e8e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Throne of Blood (Kumonosu-Jo) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260404
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c062c-d5da-4ef3-d46b-0de4f02a5d56@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 4\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN B
 Y: Hideo Oguni\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Ryuzo Kikushima\, Akira Kurosawa  \n
 WITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Isuzu Yamada\, Takashi Shimura\, Akira Kubo  \n195
 7 | 109 min. | Japan | Black-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles |
  Not Rated | 35mm\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAn adaptation of Shakespeare’s 
 Macbeth\, this jidai-geki\, or period drama\, set in feudal Japan\, is par
 t of a loose trilogy with The Lower Depths (1957\, screening Sunday\, Apri
 l 19) and The Hidden Fortress (1958\, screening Thursday\, April 23). Shak
 espeare’s plays were translated for Japanese readers as early as 1868\, 
 though they were banned during World War II for not being original Japanes
 e works\; Kurosawa wanted to take on the challenge of adapting a British s
 tory to a Japanese sensibility. With staging based in traditional Noh dram
 a and visual design inspired by Musha-e warrior paintings\, Kurosawa creat
 ed what he considered one of his most experimental works.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/throne-of-blood-kumonosu-jo-
 in-35-mm-019c062c-d5da-4ef3-d46b-0de4f02a5d56
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sanshiro Sugata with Sanshiro Sugata Part II
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260406
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0632-e620-0e6b-b515-883c7db9c068@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Kerim Yasar\, associate professor of East Asian lan
 guages and cultures\n\nSunday\, April 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1943 | 79 min. | Japan | Black-a
 nd-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED/W
 RITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWITH: Denjiro Okochi\, Susumu Fujita\, Yukik
 o Todoroki\, Ryunosuke Tsukigata \nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\
 , Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSanshiro Sugata in 35mmKurosawa’s 
 directorial debut\, adapted by the filmmaker from a book by Tsuneo Tomita\
 , deftly sets the stage for an unparalleled five-decade career. Set in 188
 2\, Sanshiro Sugata follows an aspiring martial artist determined to prove
  his worth under an impressive sensei. Strong but undisciplined\, Sugata g
 ets into scraps that sow distrust and fear in his master and community. To
 mita’s book established a story steeped in themes of honor and masculini
 ty\, and Kurosawa fleshed it out visually with organic\, fluid camerawork\
 ; staging that emphasizes depth of field\; and strong symbolism expressed 
 through the power of nature and its untamable forces.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/sanshiro-sugata-with-sanshir
 o-sugata-part-ii-019c0632-e620-0e6b-b515-883c7db9c068
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Birthday Party in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260406
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0631-f17f-75d2-8873-0ea7c06383f4@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 3m\n\n1968 | 123 min. | UK | Color | English | Rat
 ed G | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: William Friedkin \nWRITTEN BY: Harold Pinter \n
 WITH: Robert Shaw\, Patrick Magee\, Sydney Tafler\, Dandy Nichols\n\nType:
  Screenings\n\nHarold Pinter adapted his own 1957 play for this tense psyc
 hological drama\, the second film directed by Oscar winner William Friedki
 n (The French Connection\, 1971). Robert Shaw plays Stanley\, a boarding-h
 ouse resident\, whose quiet life is upended by the arrival of two sinister
  strangers. This faithful version of the stage classic\, Shaw’s second a
 ppearance in a Pinter adaptation following 1964’s The Caretaker\, gave t
 he star a rare chance to play a figure who is as much menaced as menacing\
 , and allowed moviegoers to experience the subtlety and range of his stage
  performances.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-birthday-party-in-35-mm-
 019c0631-f17f-75d2-8873-0ea7c06383f4
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:To Kill a Mockingbird in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260405
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260406
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be225-49b8-b538-9779-b453313a9b2e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Cecilia Peck\n\nSunday\, April 5\, 2026\n\nLocation
 : Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m\n\n1962 | 130 min. 
 | USA | B&W | English | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Mulligan \n
 WRITTEN BY: Horton Foote \nWITH: Gregory Peck\, Brock Peters\, Mary Badham
 \, Phillip Alford\n\nType: Screenings\n\nGregory Peck won the Best Actor O
 scar for his warm and restrained performance as attorney Atticus Finch in 
 director Robert Mulligan’s film version of Harper Lee’s classic novel 
 about racial injustice and family life in small-town Alabama. The story is
  told from the perspective of Finch’s two children\, Jem and Scout\, and
  Mulligan and uncredited casting director\, Boaty Boatwright\, found two t
 alented unknowns for the parts\, Phillip Alford and Mary Badham\, with Rob
 ert Duvall making an equally impressive film debut as the mysterious Boo R
 adley. \nThe film also won for Horton Foote’s screenplay and its black-a
 nd-white art direction\, and was nominated for Best Picture\, Mulligan’s
  directing\, Elmer Bernstein’s beautiful score\, Russell Harlan’s blac
 k-and-white cinematography\, and Badham’s effortlessly realistic perform
 ance.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/to-kill-a-mockingbird-019be2
 25-49b8-b538-9779-b453313a9b2e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Housemaid
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260406
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260407
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be236-c0f5-a6c3-91b6-44cee3f387d6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, April 6\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1960 | 108 min. | South Korea | Black-and-W
 hite | Korean with English subtitles | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Kim Ki-y
 oung \nWITH: Kim Jin-kyu\, Ju Jeung-nyeo\, Lee Eun-shim\, Um Aeng-ran \nTh
 e Housemaid was restored in 2008 by the Korean Film Archive (KOFA)\, in as
 sociation with The Film Foundation’s World Cinema Project and HFR-Digita
 l Film laboratory. Additional restoration funded by Armani\, Cartier\, Qat
 ar Airways\, and Qatar Museum Authority.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nRadical an
 d uncompromising\, Kim Ki-young’s Housemaid trilogy starts in 1960 with 
 a first chapter that continues to influence new generations of filmmakers 
 more than 60 years later. A deep survey on desire\, obsession\, and ego-dr
 iven revenge\, this domestic horror film follows a bourgeois family who hi
 res Myung-sook (Lee Eun-shim)\, an unsophisticated young woman\, as their 
 housemaid. When she becomes smitten with patriarch Dong-sik Kim\, a series
  of strange episodes unfold\, and Myung-sook’s catastrophic infatuation 
 threatens the family bond. Kim’s unconventional language of cinema depic
 ts a bleak reality that is borderline ludicrous\, in an unconventional ble
 nd of genres that redefined filmmaking in Korea.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-housemaid-019be236-c0f5-
 a6c3-91b6-44cee3f387d6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fight Club in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260408
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260409
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be20e-e790-8fb0-537b-2b697e1c3299@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: entertainment marketing executive Steve Siskind and
  Academy Governor David Dinerstein\nSelected by the Marketing and Public R
 elations Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 19m\n\n1999 | 139 min. | USA | Color | S
 cope | English | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: David Fincher \nWRITTEN BY
 : Jim Uhls \nWITH: Brad Pitt\, Edward Norton\, Helena Bonham Carter\, Meat
  Loaf Aday\n\nType: Screenings\n\nChuck Palahniuk’s bold novel inspired 
 this equally daring film version from director David Fincher\, whose choic
 es courted controversy before the work eventually earned cult-favorite sta
 tus. Edward Norton plays the unnamed hero\, who chafes against the Ikea-fu
 rnished comfort of his office-drone life only to have his world turned ups
 ide down by a chance encounter with the anarchic Tyler Durden (Brad Pitt).
  Soon the fast friends have started “Fight Club\,” an underground ente
 rprise giving suppressed men the chance to unleash their violent instincts
 . The film’s exemplary technical aspects include the Oscar-nominated sou
 nd effects editing of Ren Klyce and Richard Hymns.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/fight-club-019be20e-e790-8fb
 0-537b-2b697e1c3299
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Housemaid
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260408
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260409
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be236-e67e-aff1-6184-a875ef0a3ab9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1960 | 108 min. | South Korea | Black-an
 d-White | Korean with English subtitles | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Kim K
 i-young \nWITH: Kim Jin-kyu\, Ju Jeung-nyeo\, Lee Eun-shim\, Um Aeng-ran \
 nThe Housemaid was restored in 2008 by the Korean Film Archive (KOFA)\, in
  association with The Film Foundation’s World Cinema Project and HFR-Dig
 ital Film laboratory. Additional restoration funded by Armani\, Cartier\, 
 Qatar Airways\, and Qatar Museum Authority.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nRadical
  and uncompromising\, Kim Ki-young’s Housemaid trilogy starts in 1960 wi
 th a first chapter that continues to influence new generations of filmmake
 rs more than 60 years later. A deep survey on desire\, obsession\, and ego
 -driven revenge\, this domestic horror film follows a bourgeois family who
  hires Myung-sook (Lee Eun-shim)\, an unsophisticated young woman\, as the
 ir housemaid. When she becomes smitten with patriarch Dong-sik Kim\, a ser
 ies of strange episodes unfold\, and Myung-sook’s catastrophic infatuati
 on threatens the family bond. Kim’s unconventional language of cinema de
 picts a bleak reality that is borderline ludicrous\, in an unconventional 
 blend of genres that redefined filmmaking in Korea.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-housemaid-019be236-e67e-
 aff1-6184-a875ef0a3ab9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Drunken Angel (Yoidore Tenshi) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260408
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260409
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0633-db8a-4cd0-8734-8871f33ced20@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 38m\n\n1948 | 98 min. | Japan | Black-and-White
  | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm. \nDIRECTED BY: Akir
 a Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Keinosuke Uekusa\, Akira Kurosawa \nWITH: Taka
 shi Shimura\, Toshiro Mifune\, Reizaburo Yamamoto\, Michiyo Kogure\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\n“In this picture I finally discovered myself\,” Kurosa
 wa said in 1960 of his Occupation-period drama about a consumptive gangste
 r (Toshiro Mifune\, in a career-defining role) and the kind-hearted\, alco
 holic doctor (Takashi Shimura of Ikiru\, 1952) who treats him. Set in war-
 ravaged Tokyo and using real locations to ground the story in authenticity
 \, critics of the day noted a journalistic quality in the film\, despite t
 he director’s earlier The Most Beautiful (1944\, screening on Saturday\,
  Apr 11) also exhibiting topical themes. Widely considered one of his best
  from this period\, Drunken Angel has been compared to Billy Wilder’s Th
 e Lost Weekend (1945)\, as both interrogate the consequences of decisions 
 clouded by excessive drink.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/drunken-angel-yoidore-tenshi
 -in-35-mm-019c0633-db8a-4cd0-8734-8871f33ced20
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:No Regrets for Our Youth (Waga seishun ni kui nashi) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260409
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260410
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0634-6642-ca6a-7f2a-3129b42e2524@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, April 9\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1946 | 110 min. | Japan | Black-and-White
  | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Akira
  Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Eijirô Hisaita  \nWITH: Setsuko Hara\, Denjiro
  Okochi\, Eiko Miyoshi\, Susumu Fujita\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Founda
 tion\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFollowing the Allied occupation
  of Japan\, and in order to avoid censorship\, Kurosawa’s films began to
  reflect the newly embraced pro-democracy worldview sweeping the country. 
 These restrictions on story and theme led to the creation of one of his fe
 w female protagonists\, the complex yet relatable Yukie Yagihara\, embodie
 d by celebrated actress and frequent future Ozu collaborator Setsuko Hara 
 (Late Spring\, 1949). Beginning in the aftermath of the Mukden incident\, 
 which incited the Japanese invasion of Manchuria\, and jumping forward thr
 ough the 1930s\, Yukie navigates love\, livelihood\, family ties\, and her
  future in a film that the director later revealed is deeply nostalgic for
  his own years spent coming of age before World War II. No Regrets for Our
  Youth was released the same year as Those Who Make Tomorrow\, the only fe
 ature-length film directed by Kurosawa considered to be lost.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/no-regrets-for-our-youth-wag
 a-seishun-ni-kui-nashi-in-35-mm-019c0634-6642-ca6a-7f2a-3129b42e2524
END:VEVENT
