SUMMARY:Spirited Away
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260411
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c3013-b55b-e00d-b452-9223ae6256ac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n2001 | 125 min. | Japan | Color | English
  (Dubbed) | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki\nWITH: Dav
 eigh Chase\, Suzanne Pleshette\, Jason Marsden\, Susan Egan\n\nType: Scree
 nings\n\nSpirited Away was Hayao Miyazaki’s first Oscar win\, and the fi
 rst hand-drawn non-English-language animated film to win the Academy Award
  for Best Animated Feature in 2003. The film invites audiences into a mind
 -bending supernatural realm when a wrong turn on a family road trip lands 
 10-year-old Chihiro in a mysterious dimension populated by all manner of c
 reatures\, including soot gremlins (first introduced in My Neighbor Totoro
 )\, the giant witch Yubaba\, and\, most memorably\, the enigmatic No Face.
  To find her parents and return to the real world\, Chihiro must work at a
 n enchanted bathhouse serving spirits of all shapes and sizes. A tour de f
 orce of surrealist invention\, Spirited Away is a timeless wonder filled w
 ith whimsy and joy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/spirited-away-019c3013-b55b-
 e00d-b452-9223ae6256ac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Most Beautiful (Ichiban utsukushiku) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260411
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0639-581f-93f7-96c6-4d5b0309850e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 24m\n\n1944 | 84 min. | Japan | Black-and-White
  | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY
 : Akira Kurosawa  \nWITH: Takashi Shimura\, Soji Kiyokawa\, Ichiro Sugai\
 , Takako Irie\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nTy
 pe: Screenings\n\nKurosawa’s second feature film takes a documentary-lik
 e approach to the fictionalized story of young women working in a wartime 
 optics factory. Featuring a cast of dozens and shot on location at the Nip
 pon Kogaku factory\, The Most Beautiful experiments with narrative and exh
 ibits Kurosawa’s skill with depth-of-field cinematography within his cre
 ative use of mise-en-scène. Tinged with the visual language and conceit o
 f a Soviet-era propaganda film and steeped in wartime patriotism—especia
 lly in scenes of group activities such as volleyball matches and band prac
 tice—this portrait of female camaraderie stands out among Kurosawa’s o
 euvre for its distinct focus on women protagonists and for predating the s
 ocial realist filmmaking that would emerge in postwar Japan.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-most-beautiful-ichiban-u
 tsukushiku-in-35-mm-019c0639-581f-93f7-96c6-4d5b0309850e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Mickey 17 with Bong Joon Ho
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260413
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c7ce2-1b21-9c94-fd1a-1ddbbf2ede6b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 17m\n\n2025 | 137 min. | USA | English | Color | 
 Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Bong Joon Ho \nWITH: Robert Pattin
 son\, Naomi Ackie\, Mark Ruffalo\, Toni Collette\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBo
 ng Joon Ho’s quirky sci-fi comedy\, based on Edward Ashton’s novel Mic
 key7 (2022)\, showcases a star ensemble and lavish production design. The 
 film follows the naive Mickey Barnes (Robert Pattison)\, an “Expendable
 ” who volunteers for work that is often deadly. After every fatality\, t
 he project leaders\, an ambitious politician and his wife\, Kenneth and Yl
 fa (Mark Ruffalo and Toni Collette)\, simply print out a new Mickey and ca
 rry on—until they make a fateful mistake one day. Stunning art direction
  and crisp cinematography elevate an intriguing story marked by Bong’s s
 ignature artistry—masterful execution of continuous tonal shifts and gen
 re bending—as he underscores the sociopolitical issues often found in hi
 s work.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/mickey-17-019c7ce2-1b21-9c94
 -fd1a-1ddbbf2ede6b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Alfie in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260413
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be225-9862-2630-f28b-ee6709e16f68@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 54m\n\n1966 | 114 min. | UK | Color | Scope | Eng
 lish | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Lewis Gilbert \nWRITTEN BY: Bill Nau
 ghton \nWITH: Michael Caine\, Millicent Martin\, Vivien Merchant\, Shelley
  Winters \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nMichael Caine achieved international stardom and received his firs
 t Best Actor nomination for his wry performance as a charismatic lothario 
 in this comedy-drama from producer-director Lewis Gilbert (You Only Live T
 wice\, 1967) that is a lively time capsule of Swinging London. Gilbert and
  casting supervisor Sally Nicholl supported Caine with an impressive array
  of actresses\, including two-time Oscar winner Shelley Winters\, as well 
 as Jane Asher\, Shirley Anne Field\, and stage actress Vivien Merchant in 
 a touching\, nominated performance as one of Alfie’s married conquests. 
 \nThe film was also nominated for Best Picture (Gilbert)\, Bill Naughton
 ’s adaptation of his own play\, and the classic title song by Burt Bacha
 rach and Hal David\, performed for the film’s end credits by Cher.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/alfie-019be225-9862-2630-f28
 b-ee6709e16f68
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Quiet Duel (Shizukanaru ketto) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260413
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c063c-9531-b10b-a6c4-48e5b6611ae3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1949 | 95 min. | Japan | Black-and-White |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Ku
 rosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Kazuo Kikuta\, Senkichi Taniguchi \
 nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Takashi Shimura\, Miki Sanjo\, Kenjiro Uemura\nPri
 nt courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n
 During an urgent medical operation\, a young doctor\, Kyoji Fujisaki\, acc
 identally cuts himself and contracts syphilis\, which puts constraints on 
 his life and personal relationships\, especially with his fiancée. Toshir
 o Mifune poignantly explores Fujisaki’s agony and self-imposed isolation
  while he battles conflicting emotions of morality and desire. Kurosawa en
 hances the film with shots and depth of field that are meticulously timed 
 and framed\, and he creates a claustrophobic atmosphere by setting the act
 ion almost exclusively within the shabby confines of Fujisaki’s hospital
 .
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-quiet-duel-in-35-mm-019c
 063c-9531-b10b-a6c4-48e5b6611ae3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Men Who Tread on the Tiger’s Tail (Tora no o o wo fumu otoko
 tachi) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260412
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260413
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0639-c712-81a0-657e-c7b4f489e043@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 59m\n\n1945/1952 | 59 min. | Japan | Black-and-White
  | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY
 : Akira Kurosawa\nWITH: Denjiro Ookouchi\, Takashi Shimura\, Susumu Fujita
 \, Masayuki Mori\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\
 nType: Screenings\n\nShot exclusively on the soundstages of Toho studios\,
  Kurosawa’s fourth feature is based on a kabuki play that depicts a 12th
 -century tale of Yoshitsune\, a renowned commander of the Minamoto clan\, 
 and six samurai subordinates who disguise themselves as monks to cross ene
 my territory. Banned in postwar Japan by the US occupation for depicting f
 eudal loyalties\, this historical drama was finally screened in Japan afte
 r the Treaty of San Francisco signaled the withdrawal of US forces in 1952
 . The story is cinematically aligned with rhythmic dialogues including cha
 nting and singing executed by the incredibly talented ensemble.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-men-who-tread-on-the-tig
 ers-tail-tora-no-o-o-wo-fumu-otokotachi-in-35-mm-019c0639-c712-81a0-657e-c
 7b4f489e043
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rocco and His Brothers in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260413
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260414
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be238-35a0-48e3-d51e-df9491fbee54@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, April 13\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 59m\n\n1960 | 179 min. | Italy/France | Black-and
 -White | Italian with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP  \nDIRECTED B
 Y: Luchino Visconti  \nWRITTEN BY: Suso Cecchi D’Amico\, Pasquale Festa
  Campanile\, Massimo Franciosa\, Enrico Medioli\, Luchino Visconti  \nSTO
 RY BY: Luchino Visconti\, Suso Cecchi D’Amico\, Vasco Pratolini  \nWITH
 : Alain Delon\, Renato Salvatori\, Annie Girardot\, Katina Paxinou\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nRocco and His Brothers was a later discovery for Bong Joon
  Ho\, who put the film on his 2022 Sight and Sound list as one of the 10 f
 ilms he considers the greatest of all time. An epic melodrama from directo
 r Luchino Visconti\, the film helped to make an international star of Fren
 ch actor Alain Delon\, cast as one of five sons of a poor rural Italian fa
 mily whose fortunes fall even further after a move to Milan. The screenpla
 y was a collaboration between Visconti and four other writers\, including 
 Suso Cecchi D’Amico—one of the first female screenwriters in Italian c
 inema and a key figure in the neorealist movement\; her body of work inclu
 des such classics as Bicycle Thieves (1948).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/rocco-and-his-brothers-019be
 238-35a0-48e3-d51e-df9491fbee54
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Scandal (Skyandaru) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260415
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260416
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c6888-2e89-ac69-0317-b2356a202baa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, April 15\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mot
 ion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 44m\n\n1950 | 104 min. | Japan | Black-and-Whi
 te | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akir
 a Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Ryuko Kikushima\, Akira Kurosawa \nWITH: Toshir
 o Mifune\, Yoshiko Yamaguchi\, Takashi Shimura\, Yoko Katsuragi\nPrint cou
 rtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nReleas
 ed the same year as his celebrated Rashomon\, Kurosawa’s tenth directori
 al effort was made in response to the rise of sensational tabloid journali
 sm that paralleled Japan’s newfound concerns with freedom of speech. By 
 crafting a story that follows two famous artists who sue a gossip rag for 
 insinuating their romantic involvement\, Kurosawa hoped that Scandal would
  spark such public outcry to the distasteful trend as to quash its popular
 ity. As he noted in his 1982 memoir—and as evidenced with the later rise
  of reality television—“Scandal proved to be as ineffectual a weapon a
 gainst slander as a praying mantis against a hatchet.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/scandal-skyandaru-in-35-mm-0
 19c6888-2e89-ac69-0317-b2356a202baa
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:'Round Midnight in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260415
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260416
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be20f-3f2a-ec78-e260-05875ed0f9d5@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Music Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 15\, 2026\n\
 nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 13m\n\n1986 | 
 133 min. | France | Color | French\, English | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED
  BY: Bertrand Tavernier \nWRITTEN BY: David Rayfiel\, Bertrand Tavernier \
 nWITH: Dexter Gordon\, Francois Cluzet\, Sandra Reaves-Phillips\, Martin S
 corsese\n\nType: Screenings\n\nInternationally acclaimed filmmaker Bertran
 d Tavernier (Safe Conduct\, 2002) expressed his life-long love of jazz wit
 h this heartfelt musical drama set in 1950s Paris. French star Francois Cl
 uzet (The Intouchables\, 2011) plays a young music fan who befriends a tro
 ubled saxophone player portrayed by real-life musician Dexter Gordon\, who
  earned a Best Actor nomination for his subtle\, lived-in performance. Her
 bie Hancock\, winner of 14 Grammys\, portrayed one of the musicians and wo
 n an Oscar for the movie’s original score. Hancock also served as conduc
 tor and arranger for ’Round Midnight’s many jazz sequences.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/round-midnight-in-4-k-019be2
 0f-3f2a-ec78-e260-05875ed0f9d5
END:VEVENT
