BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:2026 Careers in Film Summit
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260418
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260419
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c6dd1-f08b-c9dd-086c-822ab4c5f7a5@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:In person: Actors Jessica Alba and Keke Palmer\n\nA free annua
 l program that raises awareness about film industry jobs for high-school a
 nd college students\, and others from under-resourced communities.\n\nSatu
 rday\, April 18\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nT
 ype: Conversations\n\nMark your calendars and join the Academy Museum on A
 pril 18 for the 2026 Careers in Film Summit\, a free annual program that r
 aises awareness about film industry jobs for high-school and college stude
 nts\, and others from under-resourced communities. Each moderated panel in
 cludes live Q&As with Academy members and other industry professionals. \n
 Enjoy demonstrations\, workshops\, complimentary professional headshots\, 
 and free entry to museum galleries with a summit ticket. American Sign Lan
 guage (ASL) interpretation is available. \nThis year\, the Academy Museum 
 welcomes actor Jessica Alba as a special guest.\nSchedule*\n10am | Museum 
 opens \n10:30am–11:15am | Welcome remarks and keynote address with Jessi
 ca Alba | Ted Mann Theater \n11:15am–4pm | Las Fotos Project portraits |
  Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby\nCome camera-ready and get a complimentary\, p
 rofessional headshot.\n11:30am–2pm | On set with the Academy Museum | Te
 a Room (5th Floor) \nDrop by\, go on set with the Academy Museum\, and dis
 cover key careers in filmmaking. Learn about on-set jobs\; experiment with
  professional equipment\; and interact with a sample production set.\n11:3
 0am and 12:30pm | Industry insights | SoC 1\nStudents can speak directly w
 ith professionals\, fielding industry insights\, tips\, and advice\, and g
 etting their questions answered. Space is limited. Program is first come\,
  first served.\n       Session 1 | 11:30am–12:15pm\n       Session 2 | 1
 2:30–1:15pm\n12:30 and 2:30pm | Voice and sound workshops | Shirley Temp
 le Education Studio \nArtists share knowledge about their career paths fol
 lowed by voice acting and sound designs demonstration. Space is limited. P
 rogram is first come\, first served.\n12:30pm–1:30pm – Voice acting wi
 th Bob Bergen (current voice of Porky Pig) \n2:30pm–3:30pm – The art o
 f Foley \n4–4:30pm | Keynote conversation with Keke Palmer  | Ted Mann T
 heater \nGain firsthand knowledge of working in the industry with a discus
 sion on finding your voice and identifying key skills for your career.\n5
 –6pm | Lights\, camera\, action! with Antigravity Academy | Ted Mann The
 ater \nThere is so much that goes into making a film that we don't see ons
 creen. Join Academy Award-nominated director Carlos López Estrada\, whose
  films include Dìdi (2024)\, Raya and the Last Dragon (2021)\, Blindspott
 ing (2018) and founder of Antigravity Academy\, a multi-hyphenate creative
  home for emerging artists across mediums\, as he explores careers in film
  with a panel of industry professionals. \n6-7pm | Coffee and career mixer
  | TMT Lobby\nMeet Academy Members\, industry professionals and members of
  community organizations\, and career opportunities they may have to share
 . Light refreshment will be available.\n6pm | Museum closes \nPLEASE NOTE:
  All summit participants\, and their guardians if participants are under t
 he age of 18\, understand that the Academy Museum has the right to photogr
 aph and film any participation in the summit. All photography and videogra
 phy will be wide-angle in nature and used solely in connection with highli
 ghting the summit on the Academy Museum website\, YouTube\, and social med
 ia channels.\nSummit tickets are available on a first-come\, first-served 
 basis and do not guarantee seating in the Ted Mann Theater\, SoC 1 or acce
 ss to the Shirley Temple Education Studio sessions.\n* Program participant
 s and programs are subject to change.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/2026-careers-in-film-summit-
 019c6dd1-f08b-c9dd-086c-822ab4c5f7a5
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:I Live in Fear (Ikimono no kiroku) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260418
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260419
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c064a-bcdc-fe1d-37a2-55f3614fb0bb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 18\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 43m\n\n1955 | 103 min. | Japan | Black-and-Whit
 e | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira
  Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Shinobu Hashimoto\, Hideo Oguni 
 \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Takashi Shimura\, Minoru Chiaki\, Masao Shimizu\n
 \nType: Screenings\n\nKurosawa contemplates the horrors of the atomic bomb
  and the fear that permeated postwar Japan in this 1955 drama. Kiichi (Tos
 hiro Mifune)\, an aging industrialist who owns a foundry\, slowly loses hi
 s grip on reality as his fear of nuclear annihilation leads him to work to
 ward relocating his family to Brazil. Alarmed by his paranoias\, the famil
 y attempts to have him declared legally incompetent. I Live in Fear is a b
 leak\, cautionary tale that explores dissonance of reality and delusion\, 
 driven by collective anxiety and instigated by isolation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/i-live-in-fear-in-35-mm-019c
 064a-bcdc-fe1d-37a2-55f3614fb0bb
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:One Wonderful Sunday (Subarashiki nichiyobi) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260419
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260420
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c068d-fb79-8423-d71b-7307313ee163@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Collection and Preservation Executive Vice 
 President Matt Severson\n\nSunday\, April 19\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1947 | 108 min. | Japan | B
 lack-and-White | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIREC
 TED BY: Akira Kurosawa  \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Keinosuke Uekusa 
  \nWITH: Isao Numasaki\, Chieko Nakakita\, Atsushi Watanabe\, Zekô Nakam
 ura\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nEngaged couple Yuzo and Masako take a weekend trip to Tokyo\, a ci
 ty recently devastated by World War II\, with little more than 35 yen—ap
 proximately $1—and the goal of having a great time. Employing sequences 
 of fantasy via the couple’s imagination\, One Wonderful Sunday is the mo
 st hopeful film from Akira Kurosawa’s early period. His fascination with
  dream life\, seen in his later Dreams (1990\, screening May 29)\, first e
 merges in this poignant\, romantic\, and hopeful vision of the future\, wh
 ich was shot entirely on location using hidden cameras with two unknown ac
 tors. The film earned Kurosawa a prize for directing at the Mainichi Film 
 Awards\, which he would later also receive for Kagemusha (1980) and Ran (1
 985).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/one-wonderful-sunday-subaras
 hiki-nichiyobi-in-35-mm-019c068d-fb79-8423-d71b-7307313ee163
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Midnight Cowboy in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260419
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260420
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be226-d097-65cf-a70e-af6a6dd5561f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 19\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 53m\n\n1969 | 113 min. | USA | Color | English | 
 Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Schlesinger \nWRITTEN BY: Waldo Salt 
 \nWITH: Dustin Hoffman\, Jon Voight\, Sylvia Miles\, Jon McGiver\n\nType: 
 Screenings\n\nDirector John Schlesinger’s film version of James Leo Herl
 ihy’s novel about two unlikely friends—a would-be gigolo and a petty h
 ustler—in the seedier side of 1960s Manhattan was a breakthrough in Amer
 ican cinema’s treatment of previously taboo subject matter. \nBest Actor
  nominations were earned by Dustin Hoffman as the ailing “Ratso” Rizzo
  and Jon Voight in a star-making turn as Joe Buck\, with supporting actres
 s nominee Sylvia Miles among the impressive cast assembled by Schlesinger 
 and pioneering\, uncredited casting director Marion Dougherty. The film ea
 rned Oscars for Best Picture (Jerome Hellman\, producer)\, Schlesinger’s
  direction\, and Waldo Salt’s screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/midnight-cowboy-019be226-d09
 7-65cf-a70e-af6a6dd5561f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Lower Depths (Donzoko) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260419
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260420
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c064b-5bcb-9df8-5424-65decc456d57@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 19\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n1957 | 125 min. | Japan | Black-and-White |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Ku
 rosawa\nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Hideo Oguni. \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\,
  Isuzu Yamada\, Kyoko Kagawa\, Ganjiro Nakamura\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKur
 osawa takes a picaresque approach in his adaptation of Maxim Gorky’s pla
 y of the same name from 1902\, with a twist of humor and joy in underlying
  despair—the premise of the work. Set in the late Edo period\, the film 
 surveys the lives of a married couple and their tenants who are barely scr
 aping by in a run-down tenement. Kurosawa uses the limited space of the te
 nement to create an intimate atmosphere for the ensemble to perform in wit
 h density that\, in turn\, resembles the anguish that each character carri
 es within them.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-lower-depths-in-35-mm-01
 9c064b-5bcb-9df8-5424-65decc456d57
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Servant in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260420
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260421
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23a-9e3f-b3b3-c986-549b2fe9d4cb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, April 20\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1963 | 116 min. | UK | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Joseph Losey  \nWRITTEN BY: Ha
 rold Pinter  \nWITH: Dirk Bogarde\, James Fox\, Sarah Miles\, Wendy Craig
 \n\nType: Screenings\n\nDirector Joseph Losey’s first of three collabora
 tions with writer Harold Pinter was this adaptation of Robin Maugham’s 1
 948 novella. Focusing on social class and interpersonal relationships\, th
 e story follows Barrett (Dirk Bogarde) who is hired as manservant when the
  wealthy Tony (James Fox) moves into a new house. The two form a bond\, bu
 t Tony is unaware of Barrett’s ulterior motive to gain the upper hand in
  their relationship. To further his goals\, Barrett convinces Tony to hire
  a maid and brings his lover (Sara Miles) into the home under the guise th
 at they are siblings.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-servant-in-4-k-019be23a-
 9e3f-b3b3-c986-549b2fe9d4cb
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Warriors
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260422
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260423
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be20f-917c-acf5-abc9-3c4c810fae88@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: producer Lawrence Gordon \nSelected by the Producer
 s Branch\n\nWednesday\, April 22\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mot
 ion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 34m\n\n1979 | 94 min. | USA | Color | English 
 | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Walter Hill \nWRITTEN BY: David Shaber\, Wa
 lter Hill \nWITH: Michael Beck\, James Remar\, Deborah Van Valkenburgh\, M
 arcelino Sanchez\n\nType: Screenings\n\nDirector Walter Hill’s action cl
 assic focuses on street toughs framed for the murder of a charismatic gang
  leader and forced to flee to their Coney Island neighborhood. Working fro
 m a shrewdly minimalist script adapted by Hill and David Shaber from the n
 ovel by Sol Yurick\, Hill and producer Lawrence Gordon (their other collab
 orations include 48 Hrs.\, 1982\, and Streets of Fire\, 1984) created a st
 ylized vision of 1970s New York that remains one of the director’s most 
 popular and influential films.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-warriors-019be20f-917c-a
 cf5-abc9-3c4c810fae88
END:VEVENT
