BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Yojimbo (Yojinbo) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260424
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260425
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c064d-0dde-ad84-61a6-a66986202520@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, April 24\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1961 | 110 min. | Japan | Black-and-White 
 | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira K
 urosawa\nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Ryuzo Kikushima\nWITH: Toshiro Mifun
 e\, Eijiro Tono\, Tatsuya Nakadai\, Yoko Tsukasa\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA 
 mysterious ronin going by the name Sanjuro Kuwabatake (Toshiro Mifune)\, w
 anders into a small town plagued by criminals. With tensions brewing betwe
 en two rival yakuza\, Sanjuro decides to clean up the village and wipe out
  the racketeers by sowing dissension among them. This samurai tale of an e
 nigmatic drifter defending a terror-stricken town\, itself a loose adaptat
 ion of pulp novelist Dashiell Hammett’s Red Harvest (1929)\, inspired a 
 mass of films following its release\, most notably the spaghetti Western A
  Fistful of Dollars (1964). The film earned an Academy Award nomination fo
 r Yoshiro Muraki’s costume design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/yojimbo-in-35-mm-019c064d-0d
 de-ad84-61a6-a66986202520
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Inside Jaws: The Art of Cinematography with Dion Beebe
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T153000
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c3574-120c-abbc-c0c1-6ee685dcd44b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:How did the makers of Jaws achieve so many iconic shots? Learn
  about the art and science of film photography with Academy member and cin
 ematographer Dion Beebe.\n\nSaturday\, April 25\, 2026 at 2pm\n\nLocation:
  Level 4\n\nCost: Free with general admission\n\nType: Conversations\n\nCi
 nematographer and Academy member Dion Beebe gives a 30-minute tour highlig
 hting the art of motion picture photography. Learn about the technical nua
 nces cinematographers consider\, including camera choice\, and how so many
  iconic shots from Jaws (1975) came together under the expertise of Bill B
 utler and his team.   \nStay for a brief Q&A with Beebe after the tour.
LOCATION:Level 4
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/inside-jaws-the-art-of-cinem
 atography-with-dion-beebe-019c3574-120c-abbc-c0c1-6ee685dcd44b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Man for All Seasons in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260425
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260426
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be271-3c35-eff7-ac05-d99ee9f001cb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 25\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h\n\n1966 | 120 min. | UK | Color | English | Rat
 ed G | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Fred Zinnemann \nWRITTEN BY: Robert Bolt \nW
 ITH: Wendy Hiller\, Leo McKern\, Robert Shaw\, Paul Scofield\n\nType: Scre
 enings\n\nRobert Bolt’s adaptation of his own Tony Award–winning play 
 tells the inspiring true story of Thomas More\, the Lord Chancellor of Eng
 land\, who risked his life in defying the will of King Henry VIII\, who de
 manded the church’s permission to divorce his first wife in order to mar
 ry Anne Boleyn. \nThe film won Oscars for Best Picture\, Directing (both f
 or producer-director Fred Zinnemann)\, Bolt’s screenplay\, Paul Scofield
 ’s performance as More\, and the film’s color cinematography and costu
 me design. Oscar winner Wendy Hiller (Separate Tables\, 1958) was nominate
 d for her touching performance as More’s wife\, and Robert Shaw earned h
 is only nomination for his charismatic performance as the mercurial Henry\
 , commanding the screen with only a few scenes.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/a-man-for-all-seasons-019be2
 71-3c35-eff7-ac05-d99ee9f001cb
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Wind Rises
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260425
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260426
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21f-0b8a-b426-a322-7ddbd1f4d76b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, April 25\, 2026\n\nLocation
 : Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 6m\n\n2013 | 126 min. |
  Japan | Color | English (Dubbed) | Rated PG-13 | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY
 : Hayao Miyazaki\nWITH: Joseph Gordon-Levitt\, John Krasinski\, Emily Blun
 t\, Martin Short\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s Oscar-nominated
  feature is a novelistic\, historical drama that unfolds over decades. Ins
 pired by the famous Italian aeronautical designer Gianni Caproni\, Jiro dr
 eams of designing and flying beautiful airplanes. Nearsighted and thus una
 ble to be a pilot\, Jiro instead becomes one of the world’s most accompl
 ished airplane designers. As war looms\, he finds his work transitioning f
 rom creating graceful flying machines to deadly tools of destruction. Inte
 rlaced with an epic romance\, The Wind Rises is a mature work from a maste
 r.\nOn the last Saturday of every month\, the museum's Family Matinee is a
 n accommodative screening where we offer open captioning\, keep the theate
 r dimly lit\, and maintain a lower volume specifically for neurodivergent 
 viewers.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-wind-rises-019be21f-0b8a
 -b426-a322-7ddbd1f4d76b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Bad Sleep Well (Warui yatsu hodo yoku nemuru) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260425
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260426
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c064d-95c8-0bd5-dbac-becff4634f3f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, April 25\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 31m\n\n1960 | 151 min. | Japan | Black-and-Whit
 e | Japanese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira
  Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Hideo Oguni\, Eijirô Hisaita\, Akira Kurosawa\, 
 Ryûkô Kikushima\, Shinobu Hashimoto  \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Masayuki 
 Mori\, Kyoko Kagawa\, Tatsuya Mihashi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBy 1960\, Kur
 osawa had been in the film industry for half his life\, excelling with con
 temporary stories (gendai-geki) and period pieces (jidai-geki) alike for T
 oho Studios\, Daiei Film\, and Shochiku Co. For The Bad Sleep Well\, the f
 ilmmaker founded Kurosawa Films\, knowing that the daring stylistic choice
 s and topical themes he wanted to explore would cause friction with the ma
 jors. A neo-noir inspired by Shakespeare’s Hamlet\, set in the corporate
  boardrooms of postwar Tokyo\, Kurosawa’s nineteenth feature again casts
  the singular Toshiro Mifune at the center of a twisted cover-up that unco
 vers the sticky topics of corruption\, shame\, and greed.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-bad-sleep-well-warui-yat
 su-hodo-yoku-nemuru-in-35-mm-019c064d-95c8-0bd5-dbac-becff4634f3f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Janie Geiser’s Time\, a substance
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260426
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260427
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21a-5319-b8b1-8b4b-c13615afb76e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmaker Janie Geiser in conversation with USC pro
 fessor Holly Willis\n\nSunday\, April 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 8m\n\nReverse Shadow \n2019. 8 min. USA.
  Color. Sound. Digital  \nAbsent Objects \n2020. 8 min. USA. Color. Sound
 . Digital \n22 Light-Years \n2021. 18 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital \nVa
 poretto  \n2021. 3 min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital \nHeliotrope \n2023. 7
  min. USA. Color. Sound. Digital  \nChameleon Law \n2022. 8 min. USA. Col
 or. Sound. Digital \nSudden Tourniquet \n2025. 8 min. USA. Color. Digital
  \nTotal program runtime: approx. 60 min.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSouthern 
 California-based artist Janie Geiser’s wide-ranging practice includes pe
 rformance\, film\, installation\, and art. Known for its recontextualizati
 on of abandoned images and objects\; embrace of artifice\; and investigati
 on of memory\, power\, and loss\, Geiser’s work “prowls around the mys
 terious boundary-land between territories—like the lands of the consciou
 s and the unconscious mind\, or life and death” (Holly Willis). The seve
 n recent works that encompass Time\, a substance span 2019–25\, and incl
 ude several that were made during the early years of the pandemic. The fil
 ms move in and out of tangible and intangible spaces\, defined by collage 
 sources including faded photographic slides\, empty photo albums\, a child
 ’s shooting game\, photographs\, negatives\, and renderings created with
  a desktop home designer program. Each film’s distinct visual and sonic 
 texture translates to a uniquely haptic experience\, with Geiser’s layer
 ing and close-ups striking a deep chord in our sense memories.  \nAll fil
 ms directed by Janie Geiser.  \nProgrammed by Hyesung ii and K.J. Relth-M
 iller
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/janie-geisers-time-a-substan
 ce-019be21a-5319-b8b1-8b4b-c13615afb76e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ordinary People in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260426
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260427
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be227-18dd-4cba-6348-0875e69008cd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, April 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1980 | 124 min. | USA | Color | English | R
 ated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Redford \nWRITTEN BY: Alvin Sargent \n
 WITH: Donald Sutherland\, Mary Tyler Moore\, Judd Hirsch\, Timothy Hutton 
 \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nR
 obert Redford made his feature directorial debut with this moving adaptati
 on of Judith Guest’s novel about the difficult relationship between an e
 motionally troubled teenager and his parents after the death of his brothe
 r. The cast\, selected by Redford and casting director Penny Perry\, balan
 ced veteran actors such as Donald Sutherland\, Mary Tyler Moore (earning a
  Best Actress nomination for a rare dramatic role)\, and Supporting Actor 
 nominee Judd Hirsch with relative newcomers such as Elizabeth McGovern\, D
 inah Manoff\, and Timothy Hutton\, who won Best Supporting Actor for his s
 ensitive performance as the guilt-ridden Conrad. The film earned additiona
 l Oscars for Best Picture (Ronald L. Schwary\, producer)\, Redford’s dir
 ection\, and Alvin Sargent’s screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/ordinary-people-019be227-18d
 d-4cba-6348-0875e69008cd
END:VEVENT
