BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Star Wars: Episode V – The Empire Strikes Back with Billy Dee Wi
 lliams
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260503
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260504
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c53c1-c681-9694-6ebb-db60f4bc2690@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actor Billy Dee Williams\n\nSunday\, May 3\, 2026\n
 \nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 7m\n\n1980 | 
 127 min | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY:
  Irvin Kershner \nWRITTEN BY: Leigh Brackett\, Lawrence Kasdan \nSTORY BY:
  George Lucas \nWITH: Mark Hamill\, Harrison Ford\, Carrie Fisher\, Billy 
 Dee Williams\n\nType: Screenings\n\nLuke Skywalker’s destruction of the 
 Death Star at the end of A New Hope managed only to slow Darth Vader’s p
 lans for galactic domination. In this highly anticipated sequel\, the youn
 g Jedi trainee and his new friends in the Rebellion face bigger challenges
 \, and Luke learns a shocking secret about his lineage in what is regarded
  as one of the greatest plot twists of all time. Though darker than the pr
 evious Star Wars film\, The Empire Strikes Back is one of the most beloved
  in the series\, and it won Oscars for its sound and visual effects. It al
 so garnered nominations for its art direction and John Williams’s score\
 , which introduced Darth Vader’s iconic theme\, “The Imperial March.
 ” \nFor our annual celebration of one of cinema’s most beloved sci-fi 
 franchises\, we’ll be joined by legendary actor Billy Dee Williams\, who
 se turn as Lando Calrissian made him the first Black actor with a major\, 
 recurring role in a Star Wars film.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/star-wars-episode-v-the-empi
 re-strikes-back-with-billy-dee-williams-019c53c1-c681-9694-6ebb-db60f4bc26
 90
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sanjuro in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260503
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260504
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c2f9a-e48b-dd48-9102-a15e629db0c5@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pi
 ctures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1962 | 95 min. | Japan | Black-and-White | Ja
 panese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm. \nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kur
 osawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Hideo Oguni\, Ryuzo Kikushima \nWITH:
  Toshiro Mifune\, Tatsuya Nakadai\, Keiju Kobayashi\, Yuzo Kayama\n\nType:
  Screenings\n\nToshiro Mifune returns as the jaded\, witty ronin Sanjuro i
 n this sequel following the massive success of its predecessor\, Yojimbo (
 1961)\, with a story adapted from a short novel by Shugoro Yamamoto. Sanju
 ro reluctantly joins a group of naive young samurai determined to fight ag
 ainst corrupt high officials\, including their own lord chamberlain. Anoth
 er masterful samurai classic by Kurosawa\, Sanjuro challenges the earnestn
 ess of the samurai genre with this playful\, accessible tale.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/sanjuro-in-35-mm-019c2f9a-e4
 8b-dd48-9102-a15e629db0c5
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seven in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260506
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260507
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be210-21c0-88ad-89bc-194de79f8e24@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Production Design Branch\n\nWednesday\, May 6\
 , 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 6m\n\
 n1995 | 126 min. | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRE
 CTED BY: David Fincher \nWRITTEN BY: Andrew Kevin Walker \nWITH: Brad Pitt
 \, Morgan Freeman\, Gwyneth Paltrow\, Richard Roundtree\n\nType: Screening
 s\n\nTwo policemen (Brad Pitt and Morgan Freeman) investigate a series of 
 brutal killings inspired by the seven deadly sins in this stylish and chil
 ling mystery that helped establish acclaimed music video director David Fi
 ncher as a major feature filmmaker. Fincher worked with an expert team to 
 bring this grim story to life\, including cinematographer Darius Khondji (
 Evita\, 1996)\, composer Howard Shore (The Silence of the Lambs\, 1991)\, 
 Oscar-nominated film editor Richard Francis-Bruce (The Shawshank Redemptio
 n\, 1994) and production designer Arthur Max (Gladiator\, 2000)\, a four-t
 ime Oscar nominee who used claustrophobic interior sets and Southern Calif
 ornia locations to create a nightmarish urban America.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/seven-in-4-k-019be210-21c0-8
 8ad-89bc-194de79f8e24
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Red Beard (Akahige) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260508
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260509
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0656-595b-c83a-0c1b-d2f649f0a9fb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pi
 ctures\n\nRuntime: 3h 5m\n\n1965 | 185 min. | Japan | Black-and-White | Ja
 panese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm. \nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kur
 osawa\nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Hideo Oguni\, Ryuzo Kikushima\, Masato
  Ide \nBASED ON A STORY BY: Shugoro Yamamoto \nWITH: Toshiro Mifune\, Yuz
 o Kayama\, Yoshio Tsuchiya\, Tatsuyoshi Ehara\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKuros
 awa and Toshiro Mifune’s grand marathon of collaborations\, which yielde
 d numerous masterpieces\, faces its finish line with a three-hour explorat
 ion of moral values and existential contemplation of humankind\, led by an
 other heartfelt performance by Mifune as a humble and compassionate clinic
  director\, aka Red Beard. He\, welcomes a young doctor\, Yasumoto (Yuzo K
 ayama)\, into his small-town practice\, only to discover that his protég
 é is arrogant\, blinded by ambition\, and reluctant to serve patients wit
 h low social standing. Kurosawa examines humanity with a thoughtful gaze\,
  gently excavating the innate goodness of humankind through lessons from b
 oth life and death.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/red-beard-019c0656-595b-c83a
 -0c1b-d2f649f0a9fb
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ponyo
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260509
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260510
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21f-9378-34da-84cd-78300fe63dc9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 9\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 43m\n\n2008 | 103 min. | Japan | Color | English (
 Dubbed) | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWITH: Cate
  Blanchett\, Noah Cyrus\, Matt Damon\, Tina Fey\n\nType: Screenings\n\nPon
 yo focuses on the friendship between 5-year-old Sosuke and a magical goldf
 ish named Brunhilde\, the young daughter of a sorcerer father and a sea-go
 ddess mother. After wandering away from her father’s four-flippered subm
 arine\, Brunhilde washes ashore and is discovered by the curious Sosuke. N
 ow renamed Ponyo\, her quick bond with Sosuke fuels her yearning to be a r
 eal girl\, and they shoulder the cost of what that might take together. Th
 e vibrant and colorful hand-drawn animations are a visual delight for audi
 ences of all ages\, as Hayao Miyazaki builds a world filled with magical g
 oldfish\, wizards\, and sea goddesses in this tale of how friendships can 
 blossom anywhere—even in the ocean’s depths.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/ponyo-019be21f-9378-34da-84c
 d-78300fe63dc9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Terms of Endearment in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260510
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260511
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be227-f630-7bc5-f8b7-e5be86b1f82f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 10\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 9m\n\n1983 | 129 min. | USA | Color | English | Rat
 ed PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: James L. Brooks \nWITH: Debra Winger\,
  Shirley MacLaine\, Jack Nicholson\, John Lithgow \nNew print courtesy of 
 the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nVeteran TV writer-produce
 r James L. Brooks made his feature directing debut with this hilarious yet
  heartbreaking adaptation of the novel by Larry McMurtry (The Last Picture
  Show\, 1971)\, following the complicated relationship between a strong-wi
 lled mother (Shirley MacLaine\, winning her only Oscar) and her daughter (
 fellow Best Actress nominee Debra Winger). Jack Nicholson won his second O
 scar for his supporting role as a free-spirited astronaut who romances Mac
 Laine\, with John Lithgow also nominated as Winger’s well-meaning suitor
 . Another member of the top-notch cast\, chosen by Brooks and legendary ca
 sting directors Ellen Chenoweth and Juliet Taylor\, was Jeff Daniels\, in 
 his breakout role as Winger’s feckless husband. \nThe film also earned t
 hree Oscars for Brooks himself—for Best Picture\, Directing\, and his ad
 apted screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/terms-of-endearment-019be227
 -f630-7bc5-f8b7-e5be86b1f82f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Wages of Fear in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260511
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260512
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23d-3b37-f09b-fc4c-081fba6522a3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 35m\n\n1953 | 155 min. | France\, Italy | Black-and
 -White | French with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY:
  Henri-Georges Clouzot \nWRITTEN BY: Henri-Georges Clouzot\, Jérôme Gér
 onimi \nWITH: Yves Montand\, Charles Vanel\, Peter van Eyck\, Folco Lulli\
 n\nType: Screenings\n\nWriter-director Henri-Georges Clouzot (Diabolique\,
  1955) adapted Georges Arnaud’s 1950 novel for this intense thriller\, w
 hich won the Golden Bear at the Berlin International Film Festival and the
  Palme d’Or at Cannes. Yves Montand plays Mario\, a playboy stuck in a r
 emote South American town. Down on his luck\, he joins three other despera
 te men in a death-defying task: driving two trucks loaded with nitroglycer
 ine over treacherous mountain roads to put out an oil well fire. Bong Joon
  Ho told an interviewer in 2020 that this was one of two films he saw when
  he was nine that inspired him to become a film director\; the other was P
 sycho (1960). William Friedkin remade the story in English in 1977 as Sorc
 erer.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/wages-of-fear-019be23d-3b37-
 f09b-fc4c-081fba6522a3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Evening of Short Films from Asian American Filmmakers
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260513
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260514
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be210-a94b-d065-dfac-b8b273f32904@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, May 13\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 6m\n\nBead Game \n1977 | 6 min. | Canada | Color | 
 English \nDIRECTED BY: Ishu Patel \nSoar \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Alyce Tzue
  \n2014 | 7 min. | USA | Color | English \nWITH: Shira Abergel\, Mason Wel
 ch \nSewing Woman \n1982 | 14 min. | USA | Black-and-White | English\nDIRE
 CTED BY: Arthur Dong \nWRITTEN BY: Lorraine Dong \nWITH: Lisa Lu \nNai Nai
  & Wài Pó \n2023 | 17 min. | USA | Color | English\, Mandarin \nDIRECTED
  BY: Sean Wang \nGwai Lo: The Little Foreigner \n2002 | 12 min. | USA | Co
 lor | English \nDIRECTED BY: Jon M. Chu \nDol (First Birthday) \n2011 | 12
  min. | USA | Color | English \nDIRECTED/WRITEN BY: Andrew Ahn \nWITH: Muk
 i Grossberg\, Joshua Kwak\, James S.W. Lee\, Martin Lee \nNami \n2015 | 13
  min. | USA | Color | English \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Masami Kawai \nWITH: 
 Kana Kawai \nWinning in America \n2021 | 10 min. | USA | Color | English \
 nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Amrita Singh \nWITH: Rithika Tudmilla\, Ranjan Singh
 \, Shiney Simon\, Rahul Tudmilla\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe Academy is cel
 ebrating Asian American\, Native American\, and Pacific Islander Heritage 
 month with this selection of short films featuring Asian American filmmake
 rs and stories. The evening includes two animated films\, Ishu Patel’s O
 scar-nominated Bead Game (1977)\, and Soar (2014)\, from filmmaker Alyce T
 zue. Arthur Dong’s Oscar-nominated documentary short\, Sewing Woman (198
 2)\, tells the life story of the filmmaker’s mother\, while Sean Wang (D
 ìdi) was nominated for his documentary Nai Nai & Wài Pó (2023)\, depict
 ing the close friendship of his elderly grandmothers. Jon M. Chu (Wicked\,
  2024) won a DGA Student Film Award for his musical short Gwai Lo: The Lit
 tle Foreigner (2002). Filmmaker Andrew Ahn (Fire Island\, 2022) made his s
 hort film Dol (First Birthday\, 2011) partly as a way of coming out to his
  parents. Masami Kawai’s Nami (2015) depicts a day in the life of a grie
 ving widow in her journey across Los Angeles. In Amrita Singh’s Winning 
 in America (2021)\, a teenage girl faces the challenges of a spelling bee 
 and her coach father.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/an-evening-of-short-films-fr
 om-asian-american-filmmakers-019be210-a94b-d065-dfac-b8b273f32904
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Wages of Fear in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260513
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260514
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23e-2972-eb73-6f3d-405c6ec15392@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, May 13\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 35m\n\n1953 | 155 min. | France\, Italy | Black-
 and-White | French with English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED 
 BY: Henri-Georges Clouzot \nWRITTEN BY: Henri-Georges Clouzot\, Jérôme G
 éronimi \nWITH: Yves Montand\, Charles Vanel\, Peter van Eyck\, Folco Lul
 li\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWriter-director Henri-Georges Clouzot (Diaboliqu
 e\, 1955) adapted Georges Arnaud’s 1950 novel for this intense thriller\
 , which won the Golden Bear at the Berlin International Film Festival and 
 the Palme d’Or at Cannes. Yves Montand plays Mario\, a playboy stuck in 
 a remote South American town. Down on his luck\, he joins three other desp
 erate men in a death-defying task: driving two trucks loaded with nitrogly
 cerine over treacherous mountain roads to put out an oil well fire. Bong J
 oon Ho told an interviewer in 2020 that this was one of two films he saw w
 hen he was nine that inspired him to become a film director\; the other wa
 s Psycho (1960). William Friedkin remade the story in English in 1977 as S
 orcerer.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/wages-of-fear-019be23e-2972-
 eb73-6f3d-405c6ec15392
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Night and the City in 35mm nitrate
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260514
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260515
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0651-3856-5dfc-6db8-c395e72fb72a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, May 14\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 36m\n\n1950 | 96 min. | UK/USA | Black-and-White 
 | English | Not Rated | 35mm nitrate \nDIRECTED BY: Jules Dassin\nWRITTEN 
 BY: Jo Eisinger\nWITH: Richard Widmark\, Gene Tierney\, Googie Withers\, H
 ugh Marlowe \n35mm nitrate collection print courtesy of the UCLA Film & Te
 levision Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAmerican director Jules Dassin’
 s British noir follows Harry (Richard Widmark)\, a small-time hustler drea
 ming of dominating the wrestling racket. When he double-crosses London’s
  underworld\, his lies become a death warrant in the city’s merciless al
 leys.\nShot in the UK while Dassin was fleeing persecution and the Hollywo
 od blacklist\, Night and the City is the first of several films he made ab
 road in Britain\, France\, and Greece. This rare original nitrate print is
  of the British release version of the film\, which runs six minutes longe
 r than the US release and features alternate opening and closing scenes as
  well as a score by prolific British composer Benjamin Frankel.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/night-and-the-city-in-35-mm-
 nitrate-019c0651-3856-5dfc-6db8-c395e72fb72a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Community Conversation: Representation in Animation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260515T133000
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019d2732-52a9-a9c4-1341-06b9de4cac7b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Angela Sanchez of LatinX in Animation and Austin Faber of Blac
 k N’ Animated discuss the past\, present\, and future of their communiti
 es’ voices in animated film.\n\nFriday\, May 15\, 2026 at Noon\n\nLocati
 on: Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\n\nLatinX in Animati
 on Development and Strategy Consultant Angela Sanchez and Black N’ Anima
 ted Director Austin Faber discuss the portrayal of their communities in an
 imated films throughout history\; the significance of authentic representa
 tion in animation\; how to support animators of color\, and more.
LOCATION:Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/community-conversation-repre
 sentation-in-animation-019d2732-52a9-a9c4-1341-06b9de4cac7b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:I Know Where I’m Going!
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260515
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260516
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0657-44a2-f54a-81a0-551563c99dd2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 15\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 32m\n\n1945 | 92 min. | UK | Black-and-White | Engl
 ish\, Scottish Gaelic | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Michael Pow
 ell\, Emeric Pressburger\nWITH: Wendy Hiller\, Roger Livesey\, Pamela Brow
 n\, Finlay Currie\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFor all of her 30 years\, nothing
  has stopped headstrong Joan Webster (Wendy Hiller) and her persistent mar
 ch toward her goals. Her latest: holy matrimony with a business magnate tw
 ice her age\, whom she hopes to wed in the Scottish Hebrides. When relentl
 ess storms prevent her from completing her journey\, she succumbs to the c
 harms of the place\, and of one local in particular\, who might be the fir
 st person ever to slow her down. \nWritten in four days and praised by Ray
 mond Chandler for its evocation of the senses\, this romantic tale reaches
  folkloric heights thanks to detailed location shooting and the unmatched 
 world-building of production designer Alfred Junge.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/i-know-where-im-going-019c06
 57-44a2-f54a-81a0-551563c99dd2
END:VEVENT
