BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Robin and Marian in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260516
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0657-fdeb-4772-f25d-e687eb4bf90e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 16\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 46m\n\n1976 | 106 min. | USA | Color | English | 
 Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Richard Lester \nWRITTEN: James Goldman \
 nWITH: Sean Connery\, Audrey Hepburn\, Robert Shaw\, Richard Harris\n\nTyp
 e: Screenings\n\nAudrey Hepburn made a welcome return to the big screen af
 ter a nine-year absence in a touching performance as Maid Marian in this r
 omantic swashbuckler with Sean Connery (at his most vulnerable) as Robin H
 ood. \nRichard Lester (The Three Musketeers\, 1973) directed the tragicomi
 c original screenplay by Oscar winner James Goldman (The Lion in Winter\, 
 1968)\, and the supporting cast features an astonishing assemblage of Brit
 ish acting talent including Richard Harris\, Denholm Elliott\, Nicol Willi
 amson\, Ian Holm\, and Robert Shaw in a quietly powerful performance as Ro
 bin’s perennial nemesis\, the Sheriff of Nottingham.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/robin-and-marian-in-4-k-019c
 0657-fdeb-4772-f25d-e687eb4bf90e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Howl’s Moving Castle
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260516
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be220-5fd1-2ac7-7ae3-ec50e4adf5b3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 16\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n2004 | 119 min. | Japan | Color | English 
 (Dubbed) | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki\nWITH: Jean
  Simmons\, Christian Bale\, Lauren Bacall\, Blythe Danner\n\nType: Screeni
 ngs\n\nAn adaptation of Diana Wynne Jones’s fantasy novel of the same na
 me\, the Oscar-nominated Howl’s Moving Castle is a pitch-perfect fairy t
 ale about aging and war\, set in a dazzling steampunk world. When young ha
 tmaker Sophie is transformed into a 90-year-old woman by the vengeful Witc
 h of the Waste\, she seeks to break the curse with the help of the enigmat
 ic wizard Howl\, the fire demon Calcifer\, and the magical moving castle t
 hey inhabit.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/howls-moving-castle-019be220
 -5fd1-2ac7-7ae3-ec50e4adf5b3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Odd Man Out
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260516
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0658-767a-4e1d-1109-50eca3c0581d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 16\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1947 | 116 min. | UK | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Carol Reed \nWRITTEN BY: F. L. Gr
 een\, R. C. Sherriff \nWITH: James Mason\, Robert Newton\, Cyril Cusack\,
  F. J. McCormick\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWounded IRA fugitive Johnny McQuee
 n (James Mason) flees through wintry Belfast\, encountering strangers whil
 e reuniting with his lover. Over 24 hours\, the exhausted McQueen displays
  the growing desperation of man with nowhere left to turn. \nShooting on t
 he streets of West Belfast in Northern Ireland and in Hackney\, London\, f
 ilmmaker Carol Reed employs the evocative locations and stark cinematograp
 hy that would come to define his later film The Third Man (1949). Few dire
 ctors in the 1940s matched Reed’s fatalistic intensity\, transforming mu
 ndane details into evocations of an unbalanced world.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/odd-man-out-019c0658-767a-4e
 1d-1109-50eca3c0581d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Black Sunday in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0659-003c-33c2-6b9a-8b303cabef76@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 23m\n\n1977 | 143 min. | USA | Color | Scope | Engl
 ish | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Frankenheimer \nWRITTEN BY: Ernes
 t Lehman\, Kenneth Ross\, Ivan Moffat \nWITH: Robert Shaw\, Bruce Dern\, M
 arthe Keller\, Bekim Fehmiu\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJohn Frankenheimer (The
  Manchurian Candidate\, 1962) directed this epic international thriller ba
 sed on the debut novel by Thomas Harris\, creator of Hannibal Lecter. Robe
 rt Shaw\, in a rare and welcome heroic leading role\, is a world-weary Mos
 sad agent on the trail of a Black September terrorist (Marthe Keller) and 
 her unstable partner (Bruce Dern) planning an unthinkable crime on US soil
 .\nJohn Williams’s score adds to the pulse-pounding excitement\, and the
  screenplay by Ernest Lehman\, Kenneth Ross\, and Ivan Moffat gives the ch
 aracters on both sides of the conflict their due.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/black-sunday-in-35-mm-019c06
 59-003c-33c2-6b9a-8b303cabef76
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Secrets & Lies
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be228-4c81-ee7e-c1b4-a971b99149cf@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 16m\n\n1996 | 136 min. | UK | Color | English | Rat
 ed R | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Mike Leigh \nWITH: Brenda Blethyn\, Mari
 anne Jean-Baptiste\, Timothy Spall\, Claire Rushbrook\n\nType: Screenings\
 n\nA woman is united with the adult daughter she never knew in this origin
 al and authentic comedy-drama from seven-time Oscar nominated writer-direc
 tor Mike Leigh. Leigh created the script by his usual method\, working wit
 h his top team of actors (assembled by casting directors Paddy Stern and S
 usie Parriss) to develop the characters and storyline\, and the result ear
 ned nominations for stars Brenda Blethyn and Marianne Jean-Baptiste as the
  unlikely mother-daughter pair. \nThe cast includes Leigh regulars such as
  Timothy Spall and Lesley Manville\, and the film was also nominated for B
 est Picture (Simon Channing-Williams\, producer)\, Leigh’s direction\, a
 nd the film’s original screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/secrets-lies-019be228-4c81-e
 e7e-c1b4-a971b99149cf
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pool of London
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260517
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260518
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0659-a2fd-1d33-5589-96ff6eeda5fd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 17\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 25m\n\n1951 | 85 min. | UK | Black-and-White | Engl
 ish | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Basil Dearden  \nWRITTEN BY: Jack Wh
 ittingham\, John Eldridge  \nWITH: Bonar Colleano\, Susan Shaw\, Earl Cam
 eron\, Renée Asherson\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis remarkable London-set c
 rime drama tells the story of Dan MacDonald (Bonar Colleano) and Johnny La
 mbert (Earl Cameron)\, two seamen entangled in a smuggling racket. Filmmak
 er Basil Dearden\, one of Britain’s finest talents\, directs with his ty
 pical rigor and makes a bold choice for its time by casting Black actor Ca
 meron as the romantic lead. Dearden lends an eerie despair to London’s w
 inding streets\, famous bridges\, dark tunnels\, and massive cathedrals by
  virtually emptying them of other people\, creating a sense of isolation t
 hat echoes the region’s prevailing postwar sentiments.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/pool-of-london-019c0659-a2fd
 -1d33-5589-96ff6eeda5fd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Passionate Friends
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260518
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260519
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065b-931a-f68a-6dc8-35c6665ebbf8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 18\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1949 | 95 min. | UK | Black-and-White | Engl
 ish | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: David Lean\nWRITTEN BY: Eric Ambler\,
  David Lean\, Stanley Haynes\nWITH: Ann Todd\, Claude Rains\, Trevor Howar
 d\, Betty Ann Davies\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBased on H. G. Wells’s story
  of former lovers who rekindle their romance in Switzerland\, this is one 
 of David Lean’s most passionate love stories\, elevated by Guy Green’s
  lyrical location cinematography in London\, Switzerland\, and France. Mar
 y Justin (Ann Todd) is married to her jealous husband\, Howard (Claude Rai
 ns)\, but finds herself besotted with old flame Steven Stratton (Trevor Ho
 ward). A continuation of the themes explored in the devastating Brief Enco
 unter (1945)\, the film embraces a female perspective\, allowing the viewe
 r to hear Mary’s interior monologue as she navigates the passion she wan
 ts and stability she needs.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-passionate-friends-019c0
 65b-931a-f68a-6dc8-35c6665ebbf8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Barton Fink in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260518
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260519
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23e-fa7c-bce5-3e19-60258b8caa53@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 18\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1991 | 116 min. | UK/USA | Color | English |
  Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Joel Coen  \nWRITTEN BY: Joel Coen\, Ethan 
 Coen  \nWITH: John Turturro\, John Goodman\, Judy Davis\, Michael Lerner 
  \nPrint courtesy of the Ed and Maya Herrera collection at the Academy Fi
 lm Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“I’ve always been drawn to the Coen
  Brothers\,” Bong Joon Ho says in the catalog for the exhibition Directo
 r’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho. “Their movies are full of misunderstand
 ings and misinterpretations.” Set in 1941\, Barton Fink is the story of 
 the titular East Coast transplant (played by John Turturro) who abandons h
 is playwrighting career to try his hand in Hollywood. During a bout of wri
 ter’s block in his dilapidated hotel\, Fink becomes acquainted with his 
 noisy neighbor (John Goodman)\, an insurance salesman whose affable appear
 ance belies his true nature. The fourth feature by the Coens was also the 
 first to win all three major prizes—Best Director\, Best Actor\, and the
  Palme d’Or—at the Cannes Film Festival. The film was nominated at the
  64th Academy Awards for Supporting Actor (Michael Lerner)\, Art Direction
 \, and Costume Design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/barton-fink-019be23e-fa7c-bc
 e5-3e19-60258b8caa53
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Being There in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260520
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260521
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be210-d889-23c4-fc46-fb61ed623eb5@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Sound Branch\n\nWednesday\, May 20\, 2026\n\nL
 ocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m\n\n1979 | 13
 0 min. | USA/West Germany | Color | English | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED B
 Y: Hal Ashby \nWRITTEN BY: Jerzy Kosinski \nWITH: Peter Sellers\, Shirley 
 MacLaine\, Jack Warden\, Melvyn Douglas\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHal Ashby d
 irected this satirical comedy-drama\, adapted by Jerzy Kosinski from his o
 wn novel\, about a sheltered gardener of little intellect (Best Actor nomi
 nee Peter Sellers\, in a subtle and remarkable comic performance) who find
 s himself among Washington\, DC\, power brokers who interpret his every be
 fuddled utterance as great wisdom.\nOscar winner Johnny Mandel (The Sandpi
 per\, 1965) composed the film’s Satie-esque score. Expert sound for this
  dialogue-driven story\, which contrasts the harsh noises of urban life wi
 th the hushed halls of power\, was the work of Oscar-nominated production 
 sound mixer Jeff Wexler and Oscar-winning re-recording mixer Donald O. Mit
 chell. Melvyn Douglas won his second Supporting Actor Oscar for his moving
  performance as the tycoon Benjamin Rand.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/being-there-019be210-d889-23
 c4-fc46-fb61ed623eb5
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Barton Fink in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260520
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260521
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23f-22c8-e9aa-fec2-4153a597de89@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, May 20\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1991 | 116 min. | UK/USA | Color | Englis
 h | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Joel Coen  \nWRITTEN BY: Joel Coen\, Eth
 an Coen  \nWITH: John Turturro\, John Goodman\, Judy Davis\, Michael Lern
 er  \nPrint courtesy of the Ed and Maya Herrera collection at the Academy
  Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“I’ve always been drawn to the C
 oen Brothers\,” Bong Joon Ho says in the catalog for the exhibition Dire
 ctor’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho. “Their movies are full of misunderst
 andings and misinterpretations.” Set in 1941\, Barton Fink is the story 
 of the titular East Coast transplant (played by John Turturro) who abandon
 s his playwrighting career to try his hand in Hollywood. During a bout of 
 writer’s block in his dilapidated hotel\, Fink becomes acquainted with h
 is noisy neighbor (John Goodman)\, an insurance salesman whose affable app
 earance belies his true nature. The fourth feature by the Coens was also t
 he first to win all three major prizes—Best Director\, Best Actor\, and 
 the Palme d’Or—at the Cannes Film Festival. The film was nominated at 
 the 64th Academy Awards for Supporting Actor (Michael Lerner)\, Art Direct
 ion\, and Costume Design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/barton-fink-019be23f-22c8-e9
 aa-fec2-4153a597de89
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Hidden Room (aka Obsession)
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260521
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260522
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065b-dc76-64a4-0aea-86ab36464280@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, May 21\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 36m\n\n1949 | 96 min. | UK | Black-and-White | En
 glish | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Edward Dmytryk\nWRITTEN/BASED ON A 
 NOVEL BY: Alec Coppel\nWITH: Robert Newton\, Sally Gray\, Naunton Wayne\, 
 Phil Brown\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA London psychiatrist (Robert Newton\, A
 round the World in 80 Days\, 1956) smuggles acid from work daily to a blit
 zed building\, with the aim of killing and dissolving the body of his wife
 ’s American lover (Phil Brown\, best known as Uncle Owen in Star Wars Ep
 isode IV: A New Hope\, 1977). Featuring a stirring score by Italian compos
 er Nino Rota (The Godfather\, 1972)\, this slow-burn relationship drama tu
 rns into a thrilling game of cat-and-mouse once Scotland Yard sets its sig
 hts on the missing American. Feverishly directed by blacklisted\, Oscar-no
 minated Edward Dmytryk (Crossfire\, 1947)\, this story is truly where Kafk
 a meets film noir.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-hidden-room-aka-obsessio
 n-019c065b-dc76-64a4-0aea-86ab36464280
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dersu Uzala in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260522
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260523
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065c-245c-46fd-e698-58e38fa15a96@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Anne McKnight\, associate professor of Japanese and
  comparative literature\n\nFriday\, May 22\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 22m\n\n1975 | 142 min. | Soviet Unio
 n/Japan | Color | Russian\, Chinese with English subtitles | Not Rated | 3
 5mm\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Yuriy Nagi
 bin \nWITH: Maksim Munzuk\, Yuri Solomin\, Mikhail Bychkov\, Vladimir Khru
 lyov \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screening
 s\n\nCaptain Arsenyev (Yuri Solomin) leads a group of Russian soldiers in 
 his expedition of the Russian Far East region in the early 1900s\, strikin
 g up an endearing friendship with a nomad hunter\, Dersu Uzala (Maksim Mun
 zuk) in the process. Based on a memoir by Vladimir Klavdiyevich Arsenyev\,
  a renowned Russian explorer whose research spanned regions of the Far Eas
 t\, Kurosawa’s epic drama contemplates the relationships between humanit
 y\, nature\, and the spiritual realm. The vastness of the Siberian forests
 \, previously unfamiliar to the Japanese master\, underscores the immense 
 power of nature and the beauty found in simplicity.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/dersu-uzala-019c065c-245c-46
 fd-e698-58e38fa15a96
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Barton Fink in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260522
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260523
