UID:019be23f-4f4e-d945-a75a-c25d2a4f792e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 22\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1991 | 116 min. | UK/USA | Color | English |
  Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Joel Coen  \nWRITTEN BY: Joel Coen\, Ethan 
 Coen  \nWITH: John Turturro\, John Goodman\, Judy Davis\, Michael Lerner 
  \nPrint courtesy of the Ed and Maya Herrera collection at the Academy Fi
 lm Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“I’ve always been drawn to the Coen
  Brothers\,” Bong Joon Ho says in the catalog for the exhibition Directo
 r’s Inspiration: Bong Joon Ho. “Their movies are full of misunderstand
 ings and misinterpretations.” Set in 1941\, Barton Fink is the story of 
 the titular East Coast transplant (played by John Turturro) who abandons h
 is playwrighting career to try his hand in Hollywood. During a bout of wri
 ter’s block in his dilapidated hotel\, Fink becomes acquainted with his 
 noisy neighbor (John Goodman)\, an insurance salesman whose affable appear
 ance belies his true nature. The fourth feature by the Coens was also the 
 first to win all three major prizes—Best Director\, Best Actor\, and the
  Palme d’Or—at the Cannes Film Festival. The film was nominated at the
  64th Academy Awards for Supporting Actor (Michael Lerner)\, Art Direction
 \, and Costume Design.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/barton-fink-019be23f-4f4e-d9
 45-a75a-c25d2a4f792e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ran (Chaos) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260523
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065c-c773-0043-1e8d-ffaa8ec242b0@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Collection and Preservation Executive Vice 
 President Matt Severson\n\nSaturday\, May 23\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 40m\n\n1985 | 160 min. | Japan | C
 olor | Japanese with English subtitles | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Ak
 ira Kurosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Hideo Oguni\, Masato Ide \nB
 ASED ON A PLAY BY: William Shakespeare \nWITH: Tatsuya Nakadai\, Akira Te
 rao\, Jinpachi Nezu\, Daisuke Ryu\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe intricate fam
 ily dynamics of Shakespeare’s King Lear are transposed to 16th-century J
 apan—a century of social unrest and near-continuous civil wars—in this
  late-career masterpiece from the 75-year-old filmmaker. After more than a
  decade of meticulous planning\, including gorgeous storyboards painted by
  the filmmaker himself\, Kurosawa’s sweeping psychological epic was made
  possible due to the financial support of the same adventurous financier w
 ho backed Spanish-Mexican filmmaker Luis Buñuel’s final five films. At 
 the time the most expensive movie ever made in Japan\, Ran also marks the 
 director’s first and only Best Directing nomination at the Oscars\; cost
 ume designer Emi Wada won for Best Costume Design\; Yoshio Muraki and Shin
 obu Muraki were nominated for Art Direction\; and Takao Saito\, Masaharu U
 eda\, and Asakazu Nakai were nominated for Cinematography.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/ran-chaos-in-35-mm-019c065c-
 c773-0043-1e8d-ffaa8ec242b0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kiki’s Delivery Service
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260523
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be21f-5322-96c7-9e6c-1577c53dc3f3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 23\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 42m\n\n1989 | 102 min. | Japan | Color | English 
 (Dubbed) | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Hayao Miyazaki \nWRITTEN BY: Eiko 
 Kadono\, Hayao Miyazaki  \nWITH: Kirsten Dunst\, Janeane Garofalo\, Phil H
 artman\, Debbie Reynolds\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA witch film like few othe
 rs\, Kiki’s Delivery Service is a warm-hearted story that follows a girl
  entering adulthood and discovering her capabilities\, while\, refreshingl
 y\, refusing romance as a central plot device. Following tradition\, on th
 e night of the full moon after she turns 13\, Kiki must take flight on her
  broom and leave her family behind to start a new life in the charming por
 t city of Koriko\, where she will learn to use her powers. With a wise-cra
 cking black cat named Jiji by her side\, enterprising Kiki quickly becomes
  a local phenomenon as she strives to find her community and herself.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/kikis-delivery-service-019be
 21f-5322-96c7-9e6c-1577c53dc3f3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Yellow Balloon
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260523
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c53be-67ce-e9e2-af0c-cb09a7d68c53@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 23\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1953 | 79 min. | UK | Black-and-White. Eng
 lish | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: J. Lee Thompson \nWRITTEN BY: Anne
  Burnaby\, J. Lee Thompson \nWITH: Andrew Ray\, Kenneth More\, Kathleen R
 yan\, Bernard Lee\n\nType: Screenings\n\nIn the bombed-out ruins of London
 \, a boy accidentally causes his friend’s death and is blackmailed by a 
 criminal into aiding a robbery. This unflinching drama marked filmmaker J.
  Lee Thompson’s breakthrough\, showcasing a bold\, gritty imagination th
 at would later be fully realized in his harrowing Cape Fear (1962). Slappe
 d with the adults-only X certificate on its initial release—meaning lead
  actor Andrew Ray wasn’t allowed to see it—the film’s most disturbin
 g scenes of childhood peril still resonate with the same tension and terro
 r as they did more than 70 years ago.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-yellow-balloon-019c53be-
 67ce-e9e2-af0c-cb09a7d68c53
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Kagemusha (The Shadow Warrior) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260525
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065d-e0d2-dc4e-d8c9-9f37095148b3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Kerim Yasar\, associate professor of East Asian lan
 guages and cultures\n\nSunday\, May 24\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 42m\n\n1980 | 162 min. | Japan | Color |
  Japanese with English subtitles | Rated PG | 35mm.\nDIRECTED BY: Akira Ku
 rosawa \nWRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\, Masato Ide \nWITH: Tatsuya Nakadai\
 , Ken’ichi Hagiwara\, Kota Yui\, Tsutomu Yamazaki \nNew print courtesy 
 of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nKurosawa faced financi
 al challenges in the 1970s\, including two unsuccessful endeavors for Holl
 ywood projects that earned him a reputation for being difficult to work wi
 th. While his regular collaborators were disengaged\, emerging filmmakers 
 in Hollywood expressed their admiration for the director\, including Georg
 e Lucas. With Lucas as executive producer\, Kagemusha resurrected Kurosawa
  from this dark period\, illuminating his keen artistry in orchestrating a
  massive period piece that infuses picturesque shots and a meditative appr
 oach to storytelling with a sense of irony.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/kagemusha-019c065d-e0d2-dc4e
 -d8c9-9f37095148b3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Erin Brockovich in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260525
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be228-9540-a09b-9917-1ac6d9d43cd6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 24\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 11m\n\n2000 | 131 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Steven Soderbergh \nWRITTEN BY: Susannah Gra
 nt \nWITH: Julia Roberts\, Albert Finney\, Aaron Eckhart\, Marg Helgenberg
 er\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJulia Roberts won the Best Actress Oscar for her
  commanding performance as the real-life legal assistant taking on a power
  company that poisoned a small California town. The film received nominati
 ons for Best Picture (Danny DeVito\, Michael Shamberg\, and Stacey Sher\, 
 producers)\, Steven Soderbergh’s direction (he won that same year for Tr
 affic\, in competition with himself)\, Susannah Grant’s original screenp
 lay\, and the supporting performance of Albert Finney as attorney Ed Masry
 \, part of the expert cast chosen by Soderbergh and casting director Marge
 ry Simkin. Additional inspired casting decisions came via Marg Helgenberge
 r as an ailing plaintiff and Aaron Eckhart as Roberts’s biker love inter
 est.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/erin-brockovich-019be228-954
 0-a09b-9917-1ac6d9d43cd6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Brighton Rock in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260524
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260525
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c065d-45d5-fcd1-36cd-b9c126339a8f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, May 24\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 32m\n\n1948 | 92 min. | UK | Black-and-White | Engl
 ish | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Boulting \nWRITTEN BY: Graham 
 Greene\, Terence Rattigan  \nBASED ON A NOVEL BY: Graham Greene \nWITH: 
 Richard Attenborough\, Hermione Baddeley\, William Hartnell\, Harcourt Wil
 liams\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA young Richard Attenborough\, embodying the 
 quintessential smooth-talking\, suave-dressing gent of British cinema\, pl
 ays Pinkie\, a hoodlum forced to marry the witness to a murder he committe
 d. The script\, based on English writer Graham Greene’s novel\, was cowr
 itten by Greene and Terence Rattigan\, one of the country’s most popular
  dramatists. \nProduced by the Boulting brothers—John would direct\, Roy
  would produce—and shot with an expressionistic\, noir style by Harry Wa
 xman (Sapphire\, 1959) on location in the seaside town of Brighton\, the p
 icture was a box office success and a critical flop\, with its more violen
 t sequences causing moral panic throughout the country—the film was even
  banned in parts of Wales.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/brighton-rock-019c065d-45d5-
 fcd1-36cd-b9c126339a8f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cure in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260525
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260526
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be23f-e508-83c4-5f7c-abd6ae912964@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, May 25\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 51m\n\n1997 | 111 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese w
 ith English subtitles | Not Rated | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Kiyoshi K
 urosawa  \nWITH: Kôji Yakusho\, Masato Hagiwara\, Tsuyoshi Ujiki\, Yukij
 iro Hotaru\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis brooding psychological crime thrill
 er introduced writer-director Kiyoshi Kurosawa to an international audienc
 e and was an influence on the wave of Japanese horror films that were popu
 lar in the late 1990s and early 2000s. Koji Yakusho stars as Takabe\, a To
 kyo detective assigned to a string of violent murders carried out by peopl
 e with seemingly no motive. While struggling to cope with his wife’s sch
 izophrenia\, Takabe find his investigation leading him to the enigmatic Ma
 miya (Masato Hagiwara)\, a former psychology student dealing with amnesia.
  Kurosawa uses thriller and horror elements to create an exploration of th
 e existential that is fraught with tension.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/cure-019be23f-e508-83c4-5f7c
 -abd6ae912964
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Hell Is a City in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260527
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260528
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0661-730d-3658-8771-a0060f094ef0@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, May 27\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 36m\n\n1960 | 96 min. | UK | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Val Guest  \nWITH: Stanle
 y Baker\, John Crawford\, Donald Pleasence\, Maxine Audley\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nHell Is a City is a superb policier featuring Stanley Baker as Ins
 pector Martineau\, who hunts a murderer (John Crawford) while grappling wi
 th his crumbling marriage in the northern town of Manchester. Soaked in a 
 jazzy score and paced with punchy dialogue\, the film is one of several in
  this program in which an American is central to the story’s primary con
 flict (see also Night and the City\, 1950\, and Obsession\, 1947).\nThis c
 rime thriller is one of more than a dozen features made by the prolific Va
 l Guest for Hammer Film Productions\, the London-based company responsible
  for the bulk of England’s genre pictures from the 1930s into the 1970s.
  The film culminates in one of British crime cinema’s most thrilling cli
 maxes\, shot on location atop a building in Manchester.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/hell-is-a-city-in-35-mm-019c
 0661-730d-3658-8771-a0060f094ef0
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Terminator 2: Judgment Day in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260527
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260528
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be211-1825-8421-38bd-c9862eb8a547@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: visual effects supervisor Dennis Muren and special 
 effects creator Shane Mahan\n\nWednesday\, May 27\, 2026\n\nLocation: Acad
 emy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 15m\n\n1991 | 135 min. | USA 
 | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: James Cameron 
 \nWRITTEN BY: James Cameron\, William Wisher \nWITH: Arnold Schwarzenegger
 \, Linda Hamilton\, Robert Patrick\, Edward Furlong\n\nType: Screenings\n\
 nArnold Schwarzenegger returned to the role that helped make him an intern
 ational movie star—an unstoppable killing machine from the future—in t
 his supersized sci-fi action sequel from writer-director James Cameron. Sc
 hwarzenegger’s Terminator is back and as fearsome as ever but with a twi
 st\; this time he’s been reprogrammed to protect Sarah Connor (Linda Ham
 ilton) and her young son John (Edward Furlong)—the future leader of the 
 human resistance against the machine-ruled dystopian Earth. The Terminator
  also faces a new and deadly foe in the liquid metal T-1000\, played by Ro
 bert Patrick with the aid of groundbreaking visual effects that won Oscars
  for Dennis Muren\, Stan Winston\, Gene Warren\, Jr. and Robert Skotak. \n
 The film also won Oscars for its makeup\, sound\, and sound effects editin
 g\, and was nominated for cinematography and film editing.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/terminator-2-judgment-day-01
 9be211-1825-8421-38bd-c9862eb8a547
END:VEVENT
