CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, May 29\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h\n\n1990 | 120 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese with 
 English subtitles | Rated PG | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Akira Kurosawa\n
 WITH: Akira Terao\, Mitsuko Baisho\, Toshie Negishi\, Mieko Harada  \nNew
  print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“Any
  description of dreams in mere words cannot capture their expressive power
 .” \n—Akira Kurosawa\, 1993  \nInspired by a passage in a novel by Fj
 odor Dostoevsky “where he talks about dreams and the fact that they expr
 ess our deepest fears and greatest hopes\,” Dreams is a visually sumptuo
 us entry in the latter half of the filmmaker’s career. Divided into eigh
 t chapters reflecting dreams from Kurosawa’s childhood through his late 
 seventies\, this impressionistic wonder was released\, appropriately\, the
  year after the director received an Honorary Oscar from the Academy “fo
 r cinematic accomplishments that have inspired\, delighted\, enriched\, an
 d entertained worldwide audiences and influenced filmmakers throughout the
  world.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/dreams-yume-in-35-mm-019c066
 1-f193-f899-3028-5842d51ed060
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Deep in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260530
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260531
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0662-8062-5739-9390-cffa085d0882@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1977 | 124 min. | USA | Color | Scope | Eng
 lish | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Peter Yates \nWRITTEN BY: Peter Benc
 hley\, Tracy Keenan Wynn \nWITH: Robert Shaw\, Jacqueline Bisset\, Nick No
 lte\, Eli Wallach\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe bestselling aquatic thriller 
 by Jaws author Peter Benchley became a summer blockbuster under the direct
 ion of four-time Oscar nominee Peter Yates (Bullitt\, 1968)\, with Robert 
 Shaw commanding the screen as a Bahamas treasure hunter who aids a vacatio
 ning couple (Jacqueline Bisset and Nick Nolte) in a dangerous hunt for a s
 unken fortune. \nThe film’s memorable diving scenes benefit from the imp
 ressive underwater cinematography of second unit directors Al Giddings (Th
 e Abyss\, 1989) and Stan Waterman\, and the evocative score by five-time O
 scar winner John Barry.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-deep-in-35-mm-019c0662-8
 062-5739-9390-cffa085d0882
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Boy and the Heron
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260530
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260531
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019be220-9c0e-c24b-a8ce-9d4d515e0c35@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, May 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: 
 Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n2023 | 124 min. | J
 apan | Color | English (Dubbed) | Rated PG-13 | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: 
 Hayao Miyazaki\nWITH: Luca Padovan\, Robert Pattinson\, Christian Bale\, G
 emma Chan\, Dave Bautista\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s latest
  Oscar-winning feature is also his most autobiographical\, inspired by Gen
 zaburo Yoshino’s 1937 novel How Do You Live? (which is the Japanese titl
 e for the film)\, and the effect World War II had on Miyazaki's childhood.
  The result is one of Miyazaki’s darkest and most mature films. After lo
 sing his mother during the war\, Mahito and his father move to the country
 side for a fresh start. Still haunted by this loss and the recent disappea
 rance of his new stepmom\, Mahito is lured to an ancient tower by a gray h
 eron and ends up in a world where the distinction between the living and t
 he dead disappears. Guided by the heron\, he must battle his grief and lea
 rn that he has the capability to create a better world.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/the-boy-and-the-heron-019be2
 20-9c0e-c24b-a8ce-9d4d515e0c35
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Madadayo (No\, Not Yet!) in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260530
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260531
DTSTAMP:20260329T015426Z
UID:019c0663-453c-82a2-5032-4355b581436b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, May 30\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 14m\n\n1993 | 134 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese
  with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Akira Kur
 osawa \nWITH: Tatsuo Matsumura\, Kyoko Kagawa\, Hisashi Igawa\, George Tok
 oro\nPrint courtesy of The Japan Foundation\, Los Angeles.\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nThe 50-year career of one of history’s greatest filmmakers concl
 udes with this heartwarming finale from 1993\, dedicated to celebrated and
  respected writer and educator Hyakken Uchida. After Uchida announces his 
 retirement from teaching\, his students host an annual gathering\, the “
 Not Yet Banquet” to celebrate his life and career. Composed of a series 
 of poignant episodes\, Kurosawa invites us into a world where beauty can b
 e found\, despite the inevitable pitfalls of reality\, in genuine connecti
 on\, loyalty\, and respect for all living creatures.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/calendar/madadayo-019c0663-453c-82a2-
 5032-4355b581436b
END:VEVENT
